"use strict";(self.webpackChunkcasdoor_website=self.webpackChunkcasdoor_website||[]).push([["41741"],{96484(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"captcha-for-login-pages","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/captcha-for-login-pages","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-captcha-for-login-pages.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-captcha-for-login-pages.mdx","title":"CAPTCHA Providers Compared for Login Pages: reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Turnstile, GeeTest and Alibaba Cloud in Casdoor","description":"Compare reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Cloudflare Turnstile, GeeTest, Alibaba Cloud Captcha and the built-in captcha for login and sign-up pages, and use them in Casdoor.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"CAPTCHA","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/captcha"},{"inline":true,"label":"reCAPTCHA","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/re-captcha"},{"inline":true,"label":"hCaptcha","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/h-captcha"},{"inline":true,"label":"Turnstile","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/turnstile"},{"inline":true,"label":"GeeTest","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/gee-test"},{"inline":true,"label":"Alibaba Cloud","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/alibaba-cloud"},{"inline":true,"label":"Security","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/security"}],"readingTime":7.73,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"captcha-for-login-pages","title":"CAPTCHA Providers Compared for Login Pages: reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Turnstile, GeeTest and Alibaba Cloud in Casdoor","description":"Compare reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Cloudflare Turnstile, GeeTest, Alibaba Cloud Captcha and the built-in captcha for login and sign-up pages, and use them in Casdoor.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","CAPTCHA","reCAPTCHA","hCaptcha","Turnstile","GeeTest","Alibaba Cloud","Security"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Testing Verification and Password Reset Emails with Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit"}},"content":"For most sign-in and sign-up pages on the global internet, Cloudflare Turnstile is the easiest pick: the free plan has no cap on challenges, and its default mode shows a checkbox only to risky visitors. If many of your users are in mainland China, use Alibaba Cloud Captcha or GeeTest instead, because Cloudflare says Turnstile is not supported there. If you need the captcha to work without any outside service, use the built-in image captcha of Casdoor. Casdoor supports all of these, plus Google reCAPTCHA (v2 and v3) and hCaptcha, as [captcha providers](/docs/provider/captcha/overview), and you pick when the captcha appears per application.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The comparison\\n\\nPricing and availability below were checked against each vendor\'s own site on 2026-11-02. Where a vendor does not publish a number, the table says so instead of guessing.\\n\\n| Provider | What the user sees | Invisible mode | Data and privacy | Free tier and pricing | Mainland China | Casdoor type |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Casdoor default | Types five digits from an image | No | Generated and checked by your Casdoor server; no third party | Free, part of Casdoor | Works, no outside call | `Default` |\\n| Google reCAPTCHA v2 | Checkbox; some users get an image challenge | v2 also has an invisible variant that challenges only suspicious traffic | Google; sets the `_GRECAPTCHA` cookie for risk analysis | Essentials tier free up to 10,000 assessments per month per organization; Premium is $8 flat up to 100,000, then $1 per 1,000 | Google says to use `www.recaptcha.net` where `www.google.com` is not accessible | `reCAPTCHA`, `reCAPTCHA v2` (checkbox) |\\n| Google reCAPTCHA v3 | Nothing; returns a score | Yes | Same as v2 | Same as v2 | Same as v2 | `reCAPTCHA v3` |\\n| hCaptcha | Checkbox with image challenges on the free plan | Passive mode on Pro (\\"99.9% passive\\") and a no-CAPTCHA mode on Enterprise | Says the free plan complies with GDPR, CCPA, LGPD and PIPL; certifies to the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework | Basic is free; Pro is $139 per month (or $99 per month billed yearly) with 100,000 evaluations, then $0.99 per 1,000; Enterprise through sales | Not documented by hCaptcha | `hCaptcha` |\\n| Cloudflare Turnstile | Managed mode shows a checkbox only when needed | Non-interactive and Invisible widget modes | Cloudflare says it processes only the data strictly necessary and does not read form entries; no Cloudflare proxy required | Free plan: unlimited challenges, up to 20 widgets; Enterprise through sales | Cloudflare: \\"not supported in Mainland China\\" | `Cloudflare Turnstile` |\\n| GeeTest CAPTCHA v4 | One-click, slide puzzle, text click, match-three | Not listed on the v4 product page | Chinese vendor; product-specific privacy policy | No public price list; 30-day free trial, then sales | Chinese vendor | `GEETEST` |\\n| Alibaba Cloud Captcha 2.0 | One-click, slider, jigsaw, image restoration | Traceless mode in the Standard and Enterprise editions | Runs on Alibaba Cloud | Pay as you go: 0.005 CNY per call in mainland China, 0.007 CNY outside; subscription editions and resource packages also available | Yes; Casdoor verifies against the Shanghai region endpoint | `Aliyun Captcha` |\\n\\nTwo notes on the reCAPTCHA rows. Google has moved reCAPTCHA into Google Cloud Fraud Defense with Essentials, Premium and Enterprise tiers, and the free allowance of 10,000 assessments is pooled across the whole Google Cloud organization. Casdoor talks to reCAPTCHA with a site key and secret key through the standard `siteverify` endpoint, and it loads the script from `recaptcha.net` rather than `google.com`, for both the browser script and server-side verification.\\n\\n## Which one to pick\\n\\n### Global SaaS\\n\\nStart with [Cloudflare Turnstile](/docs/provider/captcha/cloudflareTurnstile). The free plan does not meter challenges, the Managed mode keeps most users from clicking anything, and you do not need to route your site through Cloudflare. If you already run on Google Cloud and want reCAPTCHA\'s risk signals, [reCAPTCHA](/docs/provider/captcha/recaptcha) is fine, but watch the 10,000 free assessments: a busy sign-in page or an SMS-pumping attack can pass that in a day. [hCaptcha](/docs/provider/captcha/hcaptcha) is the other common choice; its free plan always shows a challenge, and the passive modes are paid.\\n\\n### An app with users in mainland China\\n\\nTurnstile is out, by Cloudflare\'s own documentation. For reCAPTCHA, Google documents `www.recaptcha.net` as the domain to use when `www.google.com` is not reachable, and Casdoor already uses it, but you should still test from your users\' networks before you depend on it. hCaptcha publishes no statement about China, so the same advice applies.\\n\\nThe two safe picks are [Alibaba Cloud Captcha](/docs/provider/captcha/aliyunCaptcha) and [GeeTest](/docs/provider/captcha/geetest). Alibaba Cloud publishes per-call prices and lets you start pay-as-you-go; its traceless mode (in Casdoor, use the `Popup` sub type for it) gets close to the invisible experience of Turnstile. GeeTest sells through its sales team after a 30-day trial. If you serve both China and the rest of the world from one Casdoor, remember that an application has one captcha provider, so split the audiences into two applications if they need different captchas.\\n\\n### Privacy-sensitive users in the EU\\n\\nEvery hosted captcha sends some data about the visitor\'s browser to the vendor. If your legal team wants no third party in the sign-in path at all, the Casdoor [default captcha](/docs/provider/captcha/default) is the only option in the table that keeps everything on your own server. Among the hosted ones, Turnstile and hCaptcha both describe privacy as part of the product (data limited to what the check needs, GDPR and the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework respectively). reCAPTCHA sets a `_GRECAPTCHA` cookie; Google notes that serving from `www.recaptcha.net` avoids other cookies that `www.google.com` may set. Whichever you pick, list it in your privacy notice.\\n\\n### Self-hosted, intranet or offline\\n\\nUse the default captcha. It needs no keys and no outbound network access, so it works in air-gapped installs. Pair it with the `Internet-Only` rule (below) so that people on the office network never see it, while requests from the public internet still do.\\n\\nThe default captcha is the weakest of the group against automated solvers, since it is a plain distorted-digits image. For a public, high-traffic sign-in page, combine it with [multi-factor authentication](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/mfa-methods-compared) or move users to [passwordless sign-in](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/passwordless-authentication), which removes the password guessing that captchas exist to slow down.\\n\\n## How CAPTCHA works in Casdoor\\n\\nYou add a provider with **Category** set to `Captcha` and one of these types: `Default`, `reCAPTCHA`, `reCAPTCHA v2`, `reCAPTCHA v3`, `hCaptcha`, `Cloudflare Turnstile`, `GEETEST` or `Aliyun Captcha`. Then you add it to an application on the **Providers** tab. An application can hold only one captcha provider; Casdoor refuses to save a second one. Setup steps for each vendor are in the [captcha provider docs](/docs/provider/captcha/overview).\\n\\n### Where Casdoor asks for it\\n\\nThe same provider guards several actions, and Casdoor checks the token on the server, not only in the browser:\\n\\n- Password sign-in.\\n- Sign-up form submission.\\n- Sending a verification code by email or SMS, which covers the code step of sign-up, the forgot-password page and code-based sign-in. This is the protection against bots that burn your SMS budget.\\n\\n### When Casdoor asks for it\\n\\nThe **Rule** of the captcha provider in the application decides when it appears:\\n\\n| Rule         | Behavior                                                               |\\n|---|---|\\n| `None` | Never; the captcha is off |\\n| `Dynamic` | Only after the user\'s failed sign-ins reach the application\'s **Failed signin limit** |\\n| `Always` | Every time |\\n| `Internet-Only` | For requests from public IP addresses; not for private, loopback or other non-public addresses |\\n\\n`Dynamic` is the gentlest setting for sign-in: honest users never see a challenge, and someone guessing passwords hits one after a few failures. It only applies to accounts that already exist, though. A sign-up, or a code sent to an address that has no account yet, does not trigger it. If SMS or email abuse at sign-up is your concern, use `Always` or `Internet-Only`.\\n\\n### Where it appears on the page\\n\\nOn the application\'s sign-in items, the **Captcha** item has its own rule: `Pop up` shows the captcha in a dialog when it is needed, and `Inline` puts it directly on the page. See [Change the captcha](/docs/application/signin-items-table#captcha-rules). Alibaba Cloud Captcha has an extra **Sub type**: `Embed` shows it inside the Casdoor dialog, and `Popup` hands control to Alibaba Cloud\'s own popup, which is what traceless scenes need.\\n\\nUse the **Preview** button on the provider page to check the keys before you turn the rule on.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- Google reCAPTCHA versions: https://developers.google.com/recaptcha/docs/versions\\n- Google reCAPTCHA FAQ (`recaptcha.net`, `_GRECAPTCHA` cookie): https://developers.google.com/recaptcha/docs/faq\\n- reCAPTCHA billing in Google Cloud Fraud Defense (checked 2026-11-02): https://docs.cloud.google.com/recaptcha/docs/billing-information\\n- hCaptcha pricing (checked 2026-11-02): https://www.hcaptcha.com/pricing\\n- hCaptcha privacy policy: https://www.hcaptcha.com/privacy\\n- Cloudflare Turnstile overview: https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/\\n- Cloudflare Turnstile plans (checked 2026-11-02): https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/plans/\\n- Cloudflare China Network FAQ (Turnstile in mainland China): https://developers.cloudflare.com/china-network/faq/\\n- GeeTest CAPTCHA v4 (checked 2026-11-02): https://www.geetest.com/adaptive-captcha\\n- Alibaba Cloud Captcha 2.0 overview: https://help.aliyun.com/zh/captcha/captcha2-0/product-overview/what-is-alibaba-cloud-captcha-2\\n- Alibaba Cloud Captcha 2.0 editions and prices (checked 2026-11-02): https://help.aliyun.com/zh/captcha/captcha2-0/product-overview/version-description"},{"id":"mailhog-vs-mailpit","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mailpit-vs-mailhog.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mailpit-vs-mailhog.mdx","title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Testing Verification and Password Reset Emails with Casdoor","description":"MailHog vs Mailpit: both listen on SMTP port 1025 and web UI port 8025. Compare maintenance, Docker images and test APIs, then point Casdoor at either one.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Email","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/email"},{"inline":true,"label":"MailHog","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/mail-hog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mailpit","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/mailpit"},{"inline":true,"label":"Testing","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/testing"},{"inline":true,"label":"SMTP","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/smtp"},{"inline":true,"label":"CI","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/ci"}],"readingTime":8.16,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mailhog-vs-mailpit","title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Testing Verification and Password Reset Emails with Casdoor","description":"MailHog vs Mailpit: both listen on SMTP port 1025 and web UI port 8025. Compare maintenance, Docker images and test APIs, then point Casdoor at either one.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Email","MailHog","Mailpit","Testing","SMTP","CI"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"CAPTCHA Providers Compared for Login Pages: reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Turnstile, GeeTest and Alibaba Cloud in Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/captcha-for-login-pages"},"nextItem":{"title":"MCP OAuth Redirect URIs for Cursor, Claude and ChatGPT: Registering MCP Clients in Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/mcp-oauth-redirect-uris"}},"content":"MailHog and Mailpit are both fake SMTP servers for development: they accept every message, keep it, and show it in a web UI instead of delivering it. Both use the same default ports, **SMTP on 1025 and the web UI and API on 8025**, so the SMTP address is `smtp://127.0.0.1:1025` and the inbox is at `http://127.0.0.1:8025` for either one. The difference is maintenance: MailHog\'s last release, v1.0.1, came out in August 2020, while Mailpit released v1.33.0 on October 31, 2026. For a new project, use Mailpit. Casdoor sends to both through a plain SMTP provider, so you can test sign-up codes, password reset emails and magic links without a real mail account.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## MailHog vs Mailpit at a glance\\n\\n| | MailHog | Mailpit |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Latest release | v1.0.1, 2020-08-11 | v1.33.0, 2026-10-31 |\\n| Last commit on the default branch | 2022-08-02 | 2026-10-31 |\\n| License | MIT | MIT |\\n| Default SMTP port | 1025 | 1025 |\\n| Default web UI and API port | 8025 | 8025 |\\n| Docker image | `mailhog/mailhog`, amd64 only | `axllent/mailpit`, amd64, arm64 and 386 |\\n| HTTP API | v1 and v2: list, get, delete, search by `from`, `to` or `containing` | v1: list, get, delete, search with filters such as `to:` and `subject:`, `latest` message shortcut |\\n| Default storage | In memory | Temporary SQLite database, deleted on exit |\\n| Persistent storage | MongoDB or Maildir | SQLite file (`MP_DATABASE`) or rqlite |\\n| Message limit | None documented | Keeps the newest 500 by default (`MP_MAX_MESSAGES`) |\\n| HTML check, link check, spam check | No | Yes |\\n| Webhook on new mail | No | Yes (`MP_WEBHOOK_URL`) |\\n| SMTP TLS | Not offered | Optional STARTTLS or SSL/TLS |\\n| Failure injection | Jim | Chaos |\\n| Release a message to a real SMTP server | Yes | Yes |\\n| POP3 server | No | Yes, optional |\\n| Basic auth for the web UI | Yes | Yes |\\n\\nDocker Hub counts show that many people still pull MailHog. That reflects old tutorials and CI files, not activity: an open issue on the MailHog repository titled \\"THIS PROJECT IS DEAD!\\" has a 2023 reply from the author saying he no longer has time to maintain it. Mailpit\'s README describes itself as inspired by MailHog and points to that same comment.\\n\\nThe image architecture matters more than it looks. On an Apple Silicon laptop or an ARM CI runner, `mailhog/mailhog` runs only under emulation, while `axllent/mailpit` has a native arm64 image.\\n\\n## Run either one with Docker\\n\\nMailHog:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -d --name mailhog -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 mailhog/mailhog\\n```\\n\\nMailpit:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -d --name mailpit -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 axllent/mailpit\\n```\\n\\nMailpit keeps mail in a temporary database that disappears when the container stops. To keep it across restarts, mount a volume and set `MP_DATABASE`:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -d --name mailpit \\\\\\n  -v \\"$PWD/mailpit-data:/data\\" \\\\\\n  -e MP_DATABASE=/data/mailpit.db \\\\\\n  -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 \\\\\\n  axllent/mailpit\\n```\\n\\nWithout Docker, `brew install mailhog` and `brew install mailpit` both work on macOS, and Mailpit ships static binaries for Windows, Linux and macOS on its releases page.\\n\\nIn GitHub Actions, run it as a service container next to your test job:\\n\\n```yaml\\nservices:\\n  mailpit:\\n    image: axllent/mailpit\\n    ports:\\n      - 1025:1025\\n      - 8025:8025\\n```\\n\\n## Point Casdoor at MailHog or Mailpit\\n\\nCasdoor has no separate provider type for MailHog or Mailpit, and it doesn\'t need one. The `Default` email type sends over SMTP, and both tools are SMTP servers.\\n\\n1. As a global administrator, add a provider in the `admin` or `built-in` organization.\\n1. Legen Sie **Category** auf `Email` und **Type** auf `Default` fest.\\n1. Set **Host** to where the tool runs and **Port** to `1025`.\\n1. Leave **Username** and **Password** empty.\\n1. Click **Test SMTP Connection**, then **Send Testing Email**, and check the web UI on port 8025.\\n\\nThe step-by-step pages with screenshots are [Test email with MailHog](/docs/provider/email/mailhog) and [Test email with Mailpit](/docs/provider/email/mailpit).\\n\\nA few details decide whether this works on the first try:\\n\\n**Leave SSL mode on Auto, or set it to Disable.** In `Auto`, Casdoor uses implicit TLS only on port 465 and upgrades with STARTTLS only if the server offers it. Neither MailHog nor a default Mailpit offers STARTTLS on port 1025, so the connection stays plain. Don\'t choose `Enable`: that forces implicit TLS, and the handshake fails against a plain-text server. The [SSL mode table](/docs/provider/email/overview#ssl-mode) has the full behavior.\\n\\n**Leave the credentials empty.** Casdoor logs in to the SMTP server only when **Username** is set. If you fill it in, Go\'s `PLAIN` authentication refuses to send the password over an unencrypted connection unless the host is localhost, so a MailHog at `192.168.x.x` fails with an authentication error even though MailHog would accept any login. Mailpit by default doesn\'t ask for authentication at all.\\n\\n**Use the right host.** `127.0.0.1` works only when Casdoor and the mail catcher run on the same machine. If both run in Docker Compose, use the service name, such as `mailpit`, as the host.\\n\\n**Use a global provider.** A provider owned by an ordinary organization may only reach Internet addresses, so it can\'t connect to `127.0.0.1` or a private IP. Providers in `admin` or `built-in` aren\'t restricted. See [Which addresses a provider can reach](/docs/provider/overview#which-addresses-a-provider-can-reach).\\n\\n**Set `origin` for links.** Verification codes work right away. Password reset links and magic links need Casdoor to know its public URL, through `origin` in `conf/app.conf` or a request on a loopback host. Otherwise the reset link is dropped from the email and magic links fail. See [Templates](/docs/provider/email/overview#email-content-and-placeholders).\\n\\nFinally, add the provider to your application in [Add providers to an application](/docs/application/providers) so sign-up and password reset actually use it.\\n\\n## Read the verification code in an automated test\\n\\nCasdoor\'s verification codes are six digits, and the default template reads \\"Here is your code: \\" followed by the code. An end-to-end test signs up a user, asks Casdoor to send the code, then reads it from the mail catcher\'s API.\\n\\nWith Mailpit, search by recipient and open the newest match. `GET /api/v1/search` returns matches newest first, and `GET /api/v1/message/{ID}` returns the parsed `Text` and `HTML` bodies:\\n\\n```ts\\nimport { request } from \\"@playwright/test\\";\\n\\nconst MAILPIT = process.env.MAILPIT_URL ?? \\"http://127.0.0.1:8025\\";\\n\\nexport async function latestCode(to: string): Promise<string> {\\n  const api = await request.newContext({ baseURL: MAILPIT });\\n  for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) {\\n    const res = await api.get(\\"/api/v1/search\\", {\\n      params: { query: `to:\\"${to}\\"`, limit: \\"1\\" },\\n    });\\n    const { messages } = await res.json();\\n    if (messages.length > 0) {\\n      const msg = await (await api.get(`/api/v1/message/${messages[0].ID}`)).json();\\n      const match = (msg.Text || msg.HTML).match(/code: (\\\\d{6})/);\\n      if (match) return match[1];\\n    }\\n    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 500));\\n  }\\n  throw new Error(`no verification email for ${to}`);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nIf only one test sends mail at a time, `GET /api/v1/message/latest` skips the search step. Before each test, `DELETE /api/v1/messages` with no body clears the inbox.\\n\\nWith MailHog, the closest call is `GET /api/v2/search?kind=to&query=<address>`. Messages come back newest first in `items`, and the body is in `items[0].Content.Body`:\\n\\n```ts\\nconst res = await api.get(\\"/api/v2/search\\", {\\n  params: { kind: \\"to\\", query: to, limit: \\"1\\" },\\n});\\nconst { items } = await res.json();\\nconst body: string = items[0]?.Content.Body ?? \\"\\";\\n```\\n\\n`Content.Body` is the raw MIME body. Casdoor sends quoted-printable, so `=` becomes `=3D` and long lines get soft breaks ending in `=`. Decode it first, or keep the code near the start of a short template. Clear MailHog with `DELETE /api/v1/messages`.\\n\\n## Migrating from MailHog to Mailpit\\n\\nBecause the ports match, most migrations are a one-line change in `docker-compose.yml`: replace `mailhog/mailhog` with `axllent/mailpit`. Casdoor\'s provider keeps the same host and port. What does change:\\n\\n- **Settings.** MailHog reads `MH_*` variables; Mailpit reads `MP_*`. If you used `MH_STORAGE=maildir` or MongoDB, move to `MP_DATABASE`.\\n- **Test helpers.** Mailpit has no `/api/v2` endpoints. Rewrite calls to `/api/v2/messages` and `/api/v2/search` for `/api/v1/messages` and `/api/v1/search`, and read `messages[].ID` plus `Text` or `HTML` instead of `items[].Content.Body`. The quoted-printable decoding goes away.\\n- **Search syntax.** `kind=to&query=a@example.com` becomes `query=to:\\"a@example.com\\"`.\\n- **Failure injection.** Jim has no flags in Mailpit; use Chaos.\\n- **Authentication.** If an app insists on logging in over plain SMTP, start Mailpit with `MP_SMTP_AUTH_ACCEPT_ANY=1` and `MP_SMTP_AUTH_ALLOW_INSECURE=1`. For Casdoor, leaving the credentials empty is simpler.\\n- **Retention.** Mailpit prunes to the newest 500 messages by default. Raise `MP_MAX_MESSAGES` if a long test run needs more.\\n\\n## Going to production\\n\\nMailHog and Mailpit never deliver mail to real inboxes unless you release messages by hand, so production needs a real provider. In Casdoor you swap the provider, not the templates. The email templates, the `%s` code placeholder and the `<reset-link>` block stay the same:\\n\\n- Any SMTP server with the `Default` type, described in [Email providers](/docs/provider/email/overview)\\n- [SendGrid](/docs/provider/email/sendgrid) and [Resend](/docs/provider/email/resend) through their APIs\\n- [Brevo](/docs/provider/email/brevo) through its SMTP relay\\n- [Azure Communication Services](/docs/provider/email/azureACS)\\n- Your own HTTP endpoint with [Custom HTTP Email](/docs/provider/email/customHttp)\\n\\nA common setup keeps Mailpit in development and CI and the real provider in production. Each environment points its application at a different provider, so the sign-up flow you test is the one that ships.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- MailHog repository and README: https://github.com/mailhog/MailHog\\n- MailHog configuration and API v1/v2 docs: https://github.com/mailhog/MailHog/tree/master/docs\\n- MailHog maintenance comment: https://github.com/mailhog/MailHog/issues/442#issuecomment-1493415258\\n- Mailpit repository and README: https://github.com/axllent/mailpit\\n- Mailpit documentation (Docker, storage, SMTP, webhook, search filters): https://mailpit.axllent.org/docs/\\n- Mailpit API v1 reference: https://mailpit.axllent.org/docs/api-v1/\\n- Casdoor email provider code: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/email"},{"id":"mcp-oauth-redirect-uris","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/mcp-oauth-redirect-uris","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mcp-oauth-redirect-uris.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mcp-oauth-redirect-uris.mdx","title":"MCP OAuth Redirect URIs for Cursor, Claude and ChatGPT: Registering MCP Clients in Casdoor","description":"The OAuth callback URLs that Cursor, Claude and ChatGPT use for MCP servers, where claude_desktop_config.json lives, and how to register each client in Casdoor.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"MCP","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/mcp"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"Cursor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/cursor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Claude","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/claude"},{"inline":true,"label":"ChatGPT","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/chat-gpt"},{"inline":true,"label":"Dynamic Client Registration","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/dynamic-client-registration"}],"readingTime":7.73,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mcp-oauth-redirect-uris","title":"MCP OAuth Redirect URIs for Cursor, Claude and ChatGPT: Registering MCP Clients in Casdoor","description":"The OAuth callback URLs that Cursor, Claude and ChatGPT use for MCP servers, where claude_desktop_config.json lives, and how to register each client in Casdoor.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","MCP","OAuth","Cursor","Claude","ChatGPT","Dynamic Client Registration"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Testing Verification and Password Reset Emails with Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit"},"nextItem":{"title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Protecting Any App Behind Nginx with Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/nginx-sso-authentication"}},"content":"Every MCP client sends its own redirect URI when it signs in to an OAuth-protected MCP server. Cursor desktop uses `http://localhost:8787/callback` (releases before mid-2026 used `cursor://anysphere.cursor-mcp/oauth/callback`), Cursor on the web and Cursor Cloud Agents use `https://www.cursor.com/agents/mcp/oauth/callback`, claude.ai, Claude Desktop connectors, Claude mobile and Cowork use `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`, and ChatGPT uses `https://chatgpt.com/connector_platform_oauth_redirect` or a per-connection `https://chatgpt.com/connector/oauth/<callback-id>`. If you turn on dynamic client registration in Casdoor, you don\'t enter any of these: the clients register themselves or hand Casdoor a client ID metadata document. You add them to **Redirect URLs** only when you create a Casdoor application for a client by hand.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Redirect URIs by client\\n\\n| Client | Redirect URI | How it gets a client ID from Casdoor | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Cursor desktop (current) | `http://localhost:8787/callback` | DCR, or `auth.CLIENT_ID` in `mcp.json` | Fixed port 8787 |\\n| Cursor web and Cloud Agents | `https://www.cursor.com/agents/mcp/oauth/callback` | DCR, or `auth.CLIENT_ID` | With DCR, the desktop app registers this URI and the localhost one |\\n| Cursor desktop (older releases) | `cursor://anysphere.cursor-mcp/oauth/callback` | DCR, or `auth.CLIENT_ID` | Cursor made the loopback URI the default in July 2026 |\\n| claude.ai, Claude Desktop connectors, Claude mobile, Cowork | `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback` | CIMD, then DCR, or a client ID entered in the custom connector dialog | Anthropic\'s servers make the requests |\\n| Claude Code | `http://localhost:<port>/callback` and `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback` | CIMD | The port changes per session |\\n| Claude Desktop through `mcp-remote` | `http://localhost:<port>/oauth/callback` | DCR | The port is derived from the server URL |\\n| ChatGPT | `https://chatgpt.com/connector_platform_oauth_redirect`, or `https://chatgpt.com/connector/oauth/<callback-id>` | CIMD, DCR, or a predefined client | Copy the exact value from ChatGPT |\\n\\nThese values come from the vendors\' documentation as of November 2, 2026 (see [References](#references)). Clients change them. When a sign-in fails, read the `redirect_uri` parameter of the authorization request in the browser\'s address bar and compare it with what Casdoor has.\\n\\nThe table applies in two setups: the [MCP server built into Casdoor](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/overview) at `/api/mcp`, and your own MCP server that uses [Casdoor as its authorization server](/docs/mcp-auth/overview). In both, the client finds Casdoor through protected resource metadata ([RFC 9728](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9728)) and gets its client ID from Casdoor.\\n\\n## Option 1: let clients register themselves\\n\\nCasdoor supports dynamic client registration ([RFC 7591](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7591)) at `/api/oauth/register`, and client ID metadata documents (CIMD), where the `client_id` is an HTTPS URL of a JSON document that lists the client\'s redirect URIs. One switch controls both: **Enable dynamic client registration** on the organization. It is off by default, for every organization including `built-in`.\\n\\nWith the switch on for `built-in`, the discovery document shows what the clients look for:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -s https://your-casdoor.com/.well-known/openid-configuration | jq \'{\\n  registration_endpoint,\\n  client_id_metadata_document_supported,\\n  code_challenge_methods_supported,\\n  token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported,\\n  authorization_response_iss_parameter_supported\\n}\'\\n```\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"registration_endpoint\\": \\"https://your-casdoor.com/api/oauth/register\\",\\n  \\"client_id_metadata_document_supported\\": true,\\n  \\"code_challenge_methods_supported\\": [\\"S256\\"],\\n  \\"token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported\\": [\\"client_secret_basic\\", \\"client_secret_post\\", \\"private_key_jwt\\", \\"none\\"],\\n  \\"authorization_response_iss_parameter_supported\\": true\\n}\\n```\\n\\nEach client then picks its method:\\n\\n- **Claude** uses CIMD only when the metadata has both `client_id_metadata_document_supported: true` and `none` in `token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported`. Casdoor has both once the switch is on, so Claude identifies itself with a document URL and Casdoor creates an application named `cimd_...`. Otherwise Claude falls back to DCR.\\n- **ChatGPT** prefers CIMD, with the `client_id` `https://chatgpt.com/oauth/client.json` (or a per-callback variant), and uses DCR when CIMD isn\'t available.\\n- **Cursor** registers through DCR as a public client without a secret. Cursor\'s documentation doesn\'t mention CIMD.\\n- **`mcp-remote`** registers through DCR.\\n\\nThe redirect URIs come from the registration request or the metadata document, so there is nothing to type. Registered clients run under a restricted role and their tokens can\'t call the management API. See [Dynamic client registration](/docs/application/dynamic-client-registration) for the full rules and the organization a client lands in.\\n\\n## Option 2: create an application for the client\\n\\nIf you keep registration off, create one application per client, so that you can revoke them separately:\\n\\n1. Gehen Sie in der Casdoor Admin-Console zu **Identity** > **Applications** und f\xfcgen Sie eine Anwendung hinzu, beispielsweise `cursor-mcp`.\\n1. Add the client\'s redirect URIs from the table to **Redirect URLs**. For Cursor, add all three, because different Cursor installs send different ones.\\n1. Set **Category** to `Agent`. See [Set up Casdoor for your MCP server](/docs/mcp-auth/setup) for scopes and grant types.\\n1. Give the **Client ID** to the client.\\n\\nA client secret is optional for these clients. Casdoor accepts an authorization code exchange without a secret when the request used PKCE, and Cursor, Claude and ChatGPT all use PKCE with `S256`. If a client does send a secret, it must be the right one.\\n\\nWhere the client ID goes:\\n\\n- **Cursor**: an `auth` object in `~/.cursor/mcp.json` or `.cursor/mcp.json`. With a static `CLIENT_ID`, the Cursor desktop app sends only its localhost redirect URI.\\n\\n  ```json\\n  {\\n    \\"mcpServers\\": {\\n      \\"casdoor\\": {\\n        \\"url\\": \\"https://your-casdoor.com/api/mcp\\",\\n        \\"auth\\": {\\n          \\"CLIENT_ID\\": \\"${env:CASDOOR_MCP_CLIENT_ID}\\",\\n          \\"scopes\\": [\\"application:read\\", \\"user:read\\"]\\n        }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  ```\\n\\n- **Claude**: the OAuth client ID field in the custom connector dialog. Register `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`.\\n- **ChatGPT**: ChatGPT also works with predefined OAuth clients. Copy the redirect URI from the MCP server\'s management page in ChatGPT, since which of the two forms it uses depends on the authorization server.\\n\\nStep-by-step guides: [Cursor](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/connect-cursor), [Claude Desktop](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/connect-claude-desktop), [ChatGPT](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/connect-chatgpt).\\n\\n## How Casdoor matches redirect URIs\\n\\nCasdoor compares the `redirect_uri` of a request with each entry in **Redirect URLs**:\\n\\n- The scheme, the host and the path must match. The query string isn\'t compared.\\n- The port must match, except on loopback hosts. When both URIs use the same loopback host (`localhost`, `127.0.0.1` or `::1`), Casdoor ignores the port, as [RFC 8252](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8252#section-7.3) asks for native apps. `localhost` and `127.0.0.1` are different hosts, so Claude Code needs both `http://localhost/callback` and `http://127.0.0.1/callback`.\\n- A redirect URI on a subdomain of a registered host also matches, so `https://example.com/cb` accepts `https://app.example.com/cb`. Register the most specific host you can.\\n- Custom schemes such as `cursor://` match by the same rules. `javascript:`, `data:` and `vbscript:` URIs are always rejected.\\n\\nBecause the loopback port is ignored, an `mcp-remote` or Claude Code registration keeps working when the local port changes between sessions.\\n\\n## Where claude_desktop_config.json lives\\n\\nThe [MCP documentation](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/connect-local-servers) gives these paths. In Claude Desktop, **Settings** > **Developer** > **Edit Config** opens the file and creates it if it\'s missing.\\n\\n| Operating system | Path                                                              |\\n|---|---|\\n| macOS            | `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` |\\n| Windows          | `%APPDATA%\\\\Claude\\\\claude_desktop_config.json`                   |\\n| Linux            | `~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json`                     |\\n\\nClaude Desktop reaches a remote MCP server in one of two ways, and they use different redirect URIs:\\n\\n- **As a connector**, added in Claude\'s connector settings. Anthropic\'s servers run the OAuth flow, the redirect URI is `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`, and Casdoor must be reachable from the internet.\\n- **Through `claude_desktop_config.json`**, with `mcp-remote` as a local bridge. The OAuth flow runs on your computer, and the redirect URI is `http://localhost:<port>/oauth/callback`:\\n\\n  ```json\\n  {\\n    \\"mcpServers\\": {\\n      \\"casdoor\\": {\\n        \\"command\\": \\"npx\\",\\n        \\"args\\": [\\"mcp-remote\\", \\"https://your-casdoor.com/api/mcp\\"]\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  ```\\n\\nQuit Claude Desktop completely and start it again after you edit the file.\\n\\n## Hosted clients need a public Casdoor\\n\\nChatGPT and Claude connectors call your MCP server and Casdoor from their vendors\' servers, not from your computer. Casdoor must be reachable over public HTTPS with a valid certificate. Anthropic\'s requests come from `160.79.104.0/21`, if you filter by IP. Cursor desktop, Claude Code and `mcp-remote` run the flow locally, so a Casdoor on your network works for them.\\n\\nTwo more things hosted clients check, which Casdoor already does:\\n\\n- **Issuer in the response** ([RFC 9207](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9207)): ChatGPT rejects an authorization response without the right `iss`, and uses its stable redirect URI only with authorization servers that meet its RFC 9207 requirements. Casdoor advertises the support in its metadata and adds `iss` to the redirect.\\n- **Resource indicators** ([RFC 8707](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8707)): ChatGPT sends `resource` with the MCP server URL on the authorization and token requests. Casdoor puts that value in the `aud` claim of the access token, and your MCP server should check it. See [MCP authentication](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/authentication) for the discovery flow.\\n\\nRelated reading on www.casdoor.com: [OAuth for AI agents](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/oauth-for-ai-agents) and [authorization servers for MCP](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/mcp-authorization-servers).\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- Cursor: [Model Context Protocol (MCP)](https://cursor.com/docs/mcp), section \\"Static OAuth for remote servers\\"\\n- Cursor forum: [OAuth redirect URI changed from cursor:// to http://localhost](https://forum.cursor.com/t/oauth-redirect-uri-changed-from-cursor-to-http-localhost-for-streamable-http-mcp/165019)\\n- Anthropic: [Authentication for connectors](https://claude.com/docs/connectors/building/authentication)\\n- Model Context Protocol: [Connect to local MCP servers](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/connect-local-servers)\\n- OpenAI: [Authenticate users](https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/auth)\\n- `mcp-remote`: [README](https://github.com/geelen/mcp-remote)\\n- Casdoor: [Dynamic client registration](/docs/application/dynamic-client-registration), [Set up Casdoor for your MCP server](/docs/mcp-auth/setup)"},{"id":"nginx-sso-authentication","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/nginx-sso-authentication","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/blog/2026-11-02-nginx-sso-authentication.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-02-nginx-sso-authentication.mdx","title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Protecting Any App Behind Nginx with Casdoor","description":"Add CAS, OIDC or auth_request login in front of any app behind nginx with Casdoor: forward auth, OAuth2 Proxy, an nginx module, CAS clients and MFA compared.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"nginx","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/nginx"},{"inline":true,"label":"CAS","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/cas"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"MFA","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/mfa"},{"inline":true,"label":"forward auth","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/forward-auth"}],"readingTime":10.07,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"nginx-sso-authentication","title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Protecting Any App Behind Nginx with Casdoor","description":"Add CAS, OIDC or auth_request login in front of any app behind nginx with Casdoor: forward auth, OAuth2 Proxy, an nginx module, CAS clients and MFA compared.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","nginx","CAS","SSO","OIDC","MFA","forward auth"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"MCP OAuth Redirect URIs for Cursor, Claude and ChatGPT: Registering MCP Clients in Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/mcp-oauth-redirect-uris"},"nextItem":{"title":"Spring Boot SSO with OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server and Spring Cloud Gateway with Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc"}},"content":"Open-source nginx has no CAS module, so \\"nginx CAS authentication\\" in practice means one of two setups. Either nginx asks an authentication service about every request through its `auth_request` module, and Casdoor answers (directly at `/api/forward-auth`, or through casdoor-forward-auth or OAuth2 Proxy), or the app behind nginx is itself a CAS client and talks to Casdoor\'s CAS server endpoints. If you can rebuild nginx, the Casdoor nginx module runs the login inside nginx, and if you\'d rather not run nginx at all, Casdoor\'s site proxy can sit in front of the app. In every case the login page is Casdoor\'s, so one SSO session, one set of permissions and one MFA policy cover all the apps.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The options at a glance\\n\\n| Option | Login logic runs in | What nginx needs | Protocol to Casdoor | Who gets in | Pick it when |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Casdoor forward auth | Casdoor, `/api/forward-auth` | `auth_request` | Casdoor session cookie on the app\'s domain | Application permissions, checked again at least once a minute | nginx is already in front and you want no extra service |\\n| casdoor-forward-auth | A small Go service | `auth_request` | OIDC authorization code | Casdoor roles or groups, per location | One session cookie shared by many subdomains |\\n| OAuth2 Proxy | OAuth2 Proxy | `auth_request` | OIDC | `allowed_groups`, email domains | You already run OAuth2 Proxy |\\n| casdoor-nginx-module | nginx itself | A compiled module | OIDC with PKCE, tokens verified locally | Any signed-in user of the app | No extra process, plus bearer tokens for APIs |\\n| CAS client in the app | The app | Nothing, it only proxies | CAS 1.0, 2.0, 3.0 | Whatever the app does with the attributes | The app already supports CAS |\\n| Casdoor site proxy | Casdoor on ports 80 and 443 | No nginx | Casdoor session | Application permissions | Nothing else is in front of the app |\\n\\nThe first three differ only in who answers nginx\'s subrequest.\\n\\n## Why there is no \\"nginx CAS module\\"\\n\\nCAS is a conversation between an application and the CAS server: the app sends the browser to the server\'s `/login` with a `service` URL, gets a service ticket back on that URL, and validates the ticket with a server-to-server call. Apache httpd has Apereo\'s [mod_auth_cas](https://github.com/apereo/mod_auth_cas) for doing this at the web server, but nginx has no CAS module among its official modules. For nginx, the standard building block is [`ngx_http_auth_request_module`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_auth_request_module.html): before serving a request, nginx sends a subrequest to a URL you choose. A 2xx answer lets the request through, 401 or 403 denies it with that code, and any other code is an error.\\n\\nTo the user, a CAS login and an OIDC login look the same: a redirect to the Casdoor login page and back. So for an app without CAS support, the practical answer is OIDC at the nginx layer. Background on the protocol: [What is CAS](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/what-is-cas).\\n\\nThe auth_request module is not built by default: add `--with-http_auth_request_module` when compiling, or check `nginx -V` on a packaged nginx.\\n\\n## Option 1: Casdoor\'s own forward auth\\n\\nCasdoor can answer nginx\'s subrequest itself. You add a **Site** in Casdoor whose **Domain** is the app\'s domain and whose **Casdoor app** is the application users sign in to, and add `https://app.example.com/caswaf-handler` to that application\'s **Redirect URLs**. Host and port can stay empty, since the traffic doesn\'t pass through Casdoor.\\n\\nBecause `auth_request` can\'t follow a redirect, Casdoor answers an unauthenticated subrequest with `401` and puts the login URL in `Location`. nginx turns that into a 302:\\n\\n```nginx\\nserver {\\n    server_name app.example.com;\\n\\n    proxy_buffer_size 16k;\\n    proxy_buffers 8 16k;\\n\\n    location = /caswaf-handler {\\n        proxy_pass http://casdoor:8000/api/forward-auth;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host $http_host;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Uri $request_uri;\\n    }\\n\\n    location = /casdoor-auth {\\n        internal;\\n        proxy_pass http://casdoor:8000/api/forward-auth;\\n        proxy_pass_request_body off;\\n        proxy_set_header Content-Length \\"\\";\\n        proxy_set_header X-Original-URL $scheme://$http_host$request_uri;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Method $request_method;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\\n    }\\n\\n    location / {\\n        auth_request /casdoor-auth;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_redirect $upstream_http_location;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_user $upstream_http_x_forwarded_user;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_email $upstream_http_x_forwarded_email;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_groups $upstream_http_x_forwarded_groups;\\n        error_page 401 =302 $casdoor_redirect;\\n\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-User $casdoor_user;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Email $casdoor_email;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Groups $casdoor_groups;\\n        proxy_pass http://app:3000;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe backend receives the user in `X-Forwarded-User`, `X-Forwarded-Email` and `X-Forwarded-Groups`. Access follows a [permission](/docs/permission/permission-configuration) on the application, checked again at least once a minute, so a disabled user loses access quickly. The site\'s **Public paths** (webhooks, health checks) pass without a login.\\n\\nSee [Using your own reverse proxy](/docs/site/overview#using-your-own-reverse-proxy-forward-auth) for the reference and [Add login to an app without OIDC](/docs/site/protect-app) for a Grafana walkthrough. The app must only be reachable through nginx, or anyone can send `X-Forwarded-User` themselves.\\n\\n## Option 2: casdoor-forward-auth or OAuth2 Proxy\\n\\nIf you would rather not create a Casdoor site for each app, run a small service that does the OIDC flow against Casdoor and answers the subrequest.\\n\\n[casdoor-forward-auth](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-forward-auth) is Casdoor\'s standalone service for this (it replaced traefik-casdoor-auth). It keeps the user in a signed cookie with no server state. For nginx it exposes `/verify`, which answers 200 with identity headers or 401, and nginx redirects to its `/login` on 401:\\n\\n```nginx\\nserver {\\n    server_name app.example.com;\\n\\n    location = /_casdoor_verify {\\n        internal;\\n        proxy_pass http://casdoor-forward-auth:9999/verify?groups=built-in/dev;\\n        proxy_pass_request_body off;\\n        proxy_set_header Content-Length \\"\\";\\n    }\\n\\n    location @casdoor_login {\\n        return 302 https://auth.example.com/login?rd=$scheme://$http_host$request_uri;\\n    }\\n\\n    location / {\\n        auth_request /_casdoor_verify;\\n        error_page 401 = @casdoor_login;\\n\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_user $upstream_http_x_forwarded_user;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_roles $upstream_http_x_forwarded_roles;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-User $casdoor_user;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Roles $casdoor_roles;\\n        proxy_pass http://app:8080;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe `roles` and `groups` query parameters on the verify URL restrict each app to holders of a Casdoor role or members of a group; signed-in users without them get 403. Roles and groups are read at login and kept for the session, so changes apply after the user signs in again. Setting `COOKIE_DOMAIN=example.com` lets one login cover every subdomain. The [Traefik guide](/docs/integration/go/traefik) covers the same service for Traefik.\\n\\n[OAuth2 Proxy](https://oauth2-proxy.github.io/oauth2-proxy/configuration/integrations/nginx) works the same way with `provider = \\"oidc\\"` and `oidc_issuer_url` set to your Casdoor URL. Its `/oauth2/auth` endpoint returns 202 or 401, user and email headers need `--set-xauthrequest`, and the forwarded-header handling in its nginx example requires `--reverse-proxy`. Two Casdoor settings matter: use the `JWT-Standard` token format, or users with an unverified email are rejected, and keep the `profile` scope so groups and roles are returned. See [OAuth2 Proxy with Casdoor](/docs/integration/go/oauth2-proxy) and the [nginx community edition guide](/docs/integration/C++/NginxCommunityVersion).\\n\\n## Option 3: the Casdoor nginx module\\n\\n[casdoor-nginx-module](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-nginx-module) does the login inside nginx, without a separate process. Browsers without a session go to Casdoor\'s login page; the module exchanges the code with PKCE, verifies the access token\'s signature, `exp`, `aud` and type against the Casdoor certificate, and keeps the user in a signed cookie. API clients can send `Authorization: Bearer` with a Casdoor access token, verified locally without a call to Casdoor.\\n\\nIt needs nginx 1.22 or newer and OpenSSL 1.1.1 or newer. You can build it as a dynamic module against the nginx you already run:\\n\\n```bash\\n./configure --with-compat --with-http_ssl_module --add-dynamic-module=/path/to/casdoor-nginx-module\\nmake modules\\ncp objs/ngx_http_casdoor_module.so /etc/nginx/modules/\\n```\\n\\n```nginx\\nload_module modules/ngx_http_casdoor_module.so;\\n\\nevents {}\\n\\nhttp {\\n    server {\\n        listen 443 ssl;\\n        server_name app.example.com;\\n        ssl_certificate     /etc/nginx/app.crt;\\n        ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/app.key;\\n\\n        casdoor_endpoint        https://door.example.com;\\n        casdoor_client_id       <client-id>;\\n        casdoor_client_secret   <client-secret>;\\n        casdoor_certificate     /etc/nginx/casdoor.pem;\\n        casdoor_redirect_uri    https://app.example.com/casdoor/callback;\\n        casdoor_token_location  /_casdoor/token;\\n\\n        location = /_casdoor/token {\\n            internal;\\n            proxy_pass https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/access_token;\\n            proxy_ssl_server_name on;\\n        }\\n\\n        location = /casdoor/callback {\\n            casdoor_callback;\\n        }\\n\\n        location / {\\n            casdoor on;\\n            proxy_set_header X-User $casdoor_user;\\n            proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nReplace the placeholders with the application\'s values. The session isn\'t checked with Casdoor again until it expires, so disabling a user takes effect when the session ends; `casdoor_session_timeout` shortens that window. The module has no role or group checks. On NGINX Plus, see [the NGINX Plus guide](/docs/integration/C++/Nginx) instead.\\n\\n## Option 4: the app is a CAS client\\n\\nIf the app already speaks CAS, such as a Java app with the Apereo CAS client, nginx only proxies and Casdoor is the CAS server. Each Casdoor application has CAS endpoints under `/cas/<organization>/<application>`: `/login` and `/logout`, `/validate` for CAS 1.0, `/serviceValidate`, `/proxyValidate` and `/proxy` for 2.0, `/p3/serviceValidate` and `/p3/proxyValidate` for 3.0, and `/samlValidate` for SAML 1.1 validation.\\n\\nThe app sends the browser to `/login?service=...`, Casdoor redirects back with `ticket=ST-...`, and the app validates it:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl \\"https://door.example.com/cas/my-org/my-app/p3/serviceValidate?ticket=ST-1234567890&service=https%3A%2F%2Fapp.example.com%2F\\"\\n```\\n\\nA successful answer is a `cas:serviceResponse` with `cas:user` and attributes such as email, display name and `memberOf`; add `format=json` for JSON. Things that trip people up:\\n\\n- Every `service` URL must be listed in the application\'s **Redirect URLs**, and the `service` at validation must exactly match the one the ticket was issued for.\\n- Tickets work once and expire after 5 minutes.\\n- Tickets are kept in memory, so with several Casdoor replicas, validation must reach the replica that issued the ticket (sticky sessions).\\n- Behind nginx, pass `Host` and `X-Forwarded-Proto` so the app builds the same `service` URL that the browser saw.\\n\\nThe setup, including the Java client\'s filters for each CAS version, is in [Connect a CAS client](/docs/how-to-connect/cas).\\n\\n## Option 5: let Casdoor be the reverse proxy\\n\\nWhen nothing else sits in front of the app, Casdoor can serve it directly: its site proxy listens on `gatewayHttpPort` and `gatewayHttpsPort` (80 and 443 by default) once a site exists. Set the site\'s **Host** to the backend, pick a certificate, set **Casdoor app**, and point DNS at Casdoor. Login, permissions and headers behave as in option 1. See [Sites](/docs/site/overview).\\n\\n## Adding MFA\\n\\nMFA needs no change in nginx or in the app. Casdoor asks for the second factor on its own sign-in page before it issues an OAuth authorization code or a CAS service ticket, so every option above gets it. Users can use a TOTP app, SMS, email or RADIUS, with recovery codes, and administrators can require MFA for an organization with [MFA items](/docs/organization/mfa-items). That also answers \\"CAS 2FA\\": with Casdoor as the CAS server, the second factor is part of the CAS login.\\n\\nMFA is enforced at login, not per request: a session that nginx or a forward-auth service already holds lasts until it expires, and the organization\'s **MFA remember time** lets users skip the second factor on a trusted device for a while. See the [MFA guide](/docs/user/multi-factor-authentication) and [What is MFA](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/what-is-mfa).\\n\\nOnce signed in to Casdoor, users reach other apps that send them to Casdoor without a new password prompt; with **Auto signin**, Casdoor also skips the account picker. See [single sign-on](/docs/session/single-sign-on) and [Single logout](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/single-logout).\\n\\n## Which one to pick\\n\\n- No login in the app and nginx already in front: Casdoor\'s forward auth.\\n- One login across many subdomains, or role checks per location: casdoor-forward-auth (or OAuth2 Proxy if you already run it).\\n- You build nginx yourself and also protect APIs with bearer tokens: casdoor-nginx-module.\\n- The app supports CAS: Casdoor\'s CAS endpoints, nginx as a plain proxy.\\n- No proxy yet: Casdoor\'s site proxy.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- nginx: [ngx_http_auth_request_module](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_auth_request_module.html)\\n- OAuth2 Proxy: [nginx integration](https://oauth2-proxy.github.io/oauth2-proxy/configuration/integrations/nginx)\\n- Apereo: [CAS Protocol Specification](https://apereo.github.io/cas/7.1.x/protocol/CAS-Protocol-Specification.html), [mod_auth_cas](https://github.com/apereo/mod_auth_cas)\\n- Casdoor: [casdoor-forward-auth](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-forward-auth), [casdoor-nginx-module](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-nginx-module)\\n- Casdoor docs: [Sites](/docs/site/overview), [Connect a CAS client](/docs/how-to-connect/cas), [MFA](/docs/user/multi-factor-authentication)"},{"id":"spring-boot-sso-oidc","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-spring-boot-sso-oidc.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-spring-boot-sso-oidc.mdx","title":"Spring Boot SSO with OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server and Spring Cloud Gateway with Casdoor","description":"Add single sign-on to Spring Boot with OpenID Connect and Casdoor: oauth2Login web apps, JWT resource servers and Spring Cloud Gateway TokenRelay, with YAML.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Spring Boot","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/spring-boot"},{"inline":true,"label":"Spring Security","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/spring-security"},{"inline":true,"label":"Spring Cloud Gateway","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/spring-cloud-gateway"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth 2.0","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/o-auth-2-0"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"JWT","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/jwt"}],"readingTime":11.76,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"spring-boot-sso-oidc","title":"Spring Boot SSO with OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server and Spring Cloud Gateway with Casdoor","description":"Add single sign-on to Spring Boot with OpenID Connect and Casdoor: oauth2Login web apps, JWT resource servers and Spring Cloud Gateway TokenRelay, with YAML.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Spring Boot","Spring Security","Spring Cloud Gateway","OIDC","OAuth 2.0","SSO","JWT"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Protecting Any App Behind Nginx with Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/nginx-sso-authentication"},"nextItem":{"title":"Casdoor v4: Eine neue Konsole, ein Marktplatz und OAuth f\xfcr MCP-Server","permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-v4"}},"content":"To add single sign-on to Spring Boot, register each app as an application in an OpenID Connect provider such as Casdoor, point Spring Security at the provider\'s issuer URL, and pick the Spring module that matches the app: `oauth2Login()` for a server-rendered web app, `oauth2ResourceServer().jwt()` for a REST API that receives bearer tokens, and Spring Cloud Gateway with the `TokenRelay` filter when one gateway signs users in for a set of microservices. Users get SSO because every app sends them to the same Casdoor, which remembers the session: the second app redirects, Casdoor already knows the user, and they come straight back without a login form. This post sets up all three shapes against one Casdoor, each with a working `application.yml` and a customized `SecurityFilterChain`.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The three shapes at a glance\\n\\n| Shape | Spring dependency | Who holds the login | Casdoor endpoints used | Filter chain |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Web app | `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client` | The app (HTTP session) | discovery, authorize, token, userinfo, JWKS, logout | `SecurityFilterChain` with `oauth2Login()` |\\n| REST API | `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server` | Nobody, every request carries a bearer token | JWKS (and discovery if you use `issuer-uri`) | `SecurityFilterChain` with `oauth2ResourceServer()` |\\n| Gateway + microservices | `spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux` + `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client` | The gateway (WebFlux session) | Same as the web app; services behind it use JWKS | `SecurityWebFilterChain` with `oauth2Login()`, `TokenRelay` on routes |\\n\\nVersion assumptions: Spring Boot 3.5 with Spring Security 6.5, Java 17, and Spring Cloud 2025.0 (Spring Cloud Gateway 4.3). On Spring Boot 4 the two Security starters are named `spring-boot-starter-security-oauth2-client` and `spring-boot-starter-security-oauth2-resource-server`. Gateway 4.3 and 5.0 both use the `spring.cloud.gateway.server.webflux.*` property prefix used below; the older `spring.cloud.gateway.*` prefix and the `spring-cloud-starter-gateway` artifact are deprecated since Spring Cloud 2025.0.\\n\\n## What Casdoor gives Spring\\n\\nIn the Casdoor admin console, create an application (or reuse one) and copy its **Client ID** and **Client secret**. Add every callback the Spring apps will use to **Redirect URLs**. Spring Security\'s default is `{baseUrl}/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}`, so with the registration ID `casdoor` and an app on port 8080 the URL is `http://localhost:8080/login/oauth2/code/casdoor`. If you use OIDC logout, also add the page users land on afterwards, for example `http://localhost:8080/`; Casdoor refuses a `post_logout_redirect_uri` that is not in the list.\\n\\nCasdoor publishes a standard discovery document at `/.well-known/openid-configuration`, so Spring only needs the issuer. These are the endpoints it advertises, taken from the Casdoor source:\\n\\n| Metadata field | Casdoor path |\\n|---|---|\\n| `issuer` | The backend origin, e.g. `https://door.example.com` (no trailing slash) |\\n| `authorization_endpoint` | `/login/oauth/authorize` |\\n| `token_endpoint` | `/api/login/oauth/access_token` |\\n| `userinfo_endpoint` | `/api/userinfo` |\\n| `jwks_uri` | `/.well-known/jwks` |\\n| `end_session_endpoint` | `/api/logout` |\\n\\nSupported scopes are `openid`, `email`, `profile`, `address`, `phone` and `offline_access`, plus any custom scopes defined on the application. Tokens are signed with RS256 by default and carry the cert name as `kid`. Each application also has its own discovery document at `/.well-known/<application>/openid-configuration`; see [Connect a standard OIDC client](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client) for the details and the full list of [OAuth 2.0 endpoints](/docs/how-to-connect/oauth).\\n\\nTwo things in Casdoor\'s tokens matter for Spring:\\n\\n- **The issuer must match exactly.** Spring compares the `issuer` in the discovery document with the `issuer-uri` you configure, and later with the `iss` claim of every token. Casdoor derives the issuer from the `origin` setting in `app.conf`, or from the Host header when `origin` is empty. If your Spring apps reach Casdoor through an internal hostname (say `http://casdoor:8000` inside Docker) while browsers use a public one, set `origin` to the public URL so every token gets the same `iss`. See [Configuration](/docs/basic/configuration).\\n- **Roles come in two forms.** `/api/userinfo` returns `roles` as a list of role names when the `profile` scope was granted. The access token in the default `JWT` [token format](/docs/token/overview) contains `roles` as a list of role objects, each with a `name` field. The code below handles both. Casdoor access tokens also carry `scope` as a space-separated string, which Spring turns into `SCOPE_...` authorities by default.\\n\\nIf you want background on the protocol, see [What is OpenID Connect](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/what-is-openid-connect) and [ID token vs access token](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/id-token-vs-access-token).\\n\\n## 1. Spring Boot web app with oauth2Login()\\n\\nAdd `spring-boot-starter-web`, `spring-boot-starter-security` and `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client`. Then configure the provider by issuer:\\n\\n```yaml title=\\"application.yml\\"\\nserver:\\n  port: 8080\\n\\nspring:\\n  security:\\n    oauth2:\\n      client:\\n        registration:\\n          casdoor:\\n            client-id: <client-id>\\n            client-secret: <client-secret>\\n            authorization-grant-type: authorization_code\\n            scope: openid,profile,email\\n        provider:\\n          casdoor:\\n            issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n            user-name-attribute: preferred_username\\n```\\n\\nAt startup Spring Boot fetches `https://door.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration` and fills in the authorization, token, userinfo and JWKS URIs. Spring then validates the ID token\'s signature, issuer, audience and nonce, calls `/api/userinfo` because `profile` and `email` are in the scope, and merges the claims. `preferred_username` is the Casdoor user name; `name` in userinfo is the display name, and the default `sub` is the user\'s UUID.\\n\\nWithout your own configuration, Spring Boot requires login for every URL. The customized `SecurityFilterChain` below opens the home page, maps Casdoor roles to `ROLE_` authorities, and logs out of Casdoor as well as the app:\\n\\n```java title=\\"SecurityConfig.java\\"\\n@Configuration\\n@EnableWebSecurity\\npublic class SecurityConfig {\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http,\\n            ClientRegistrationRepository clientRegistrations) throws Exception {\\n        OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler oidcLogout =\\n                new OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler(clientRegistrations);\\n        oidcLogout.setPostLogoutRedirectUri(\\"{baseUrl}/\\");\\n\\n        http\\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/\\", \\"/public/**\\", \\"/error\\").permitAll()\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/admin/**\\").hasRole(\\"admin\\")\\n                .anyRequest().authenticated())\\n            .oauth2Login(login -> login\\n                .userInfoEndpoint(userInfo -> userInfo\\n                    .userAuthoritiesMapper(casdoorRoles())))\\n            .logout(logout -> logout\\n                .logoutSuccessHandler(oidcLogout));\\n        return http.build();\\n    }\\n\\n    private GrantedAuthoritiesMapper casdoorRoles() {\\n        return authorities -> {\\n            Set<GrantedAuthority> mapped = new HashSet<>(authorities);\\n            for (GrantedAuthority authority : authorities) {\\n                if (authority instanceof OidcUserAuthority oidc && oidc.getUserInfo() != null) {\\n                    List<String> roles = oidc.getUserInfo().getClaimAsStringList(\\"roles\\");\\n                    if (roles != null) {\\n                        roles.forEach(role -> mapped.add(new SimpleGrantedAuthority(\\"ROLE_\\" + role)));\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n            return mapped;\\n        };\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n`hasRole(\\"admin\\")` now matches users who have the Casdoor role `admin`. `OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler` reads `end_session_endpoint` from the discovery document and redirects to Casdoor\'s `/api/logout` with `id_token_hint` and `post_logout_redirect_uri`, so the Casdoor session ends too. Without it, `/logout` only clears the Spring session and the next login is silent.\\n\\nIn a controller, the signed-in user is an `OidcUser`:\\n\\n```java\\n@GetMapping(\\"/me\\")\\nMap<String, Object> me(@AuthenticationPrincipal OidcUser user) {\\n    return Map.of(\\"name\\", user.getName(), \\"email\\", user.getEmail(),\\n            \\"authorities\\", user.getAuthorities().toString());\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe older step-by-step page for this setup is [Spring Security OAuth](/docs/integration/java/spring-security/spring-security-oauth).\\n\\n## 2. Spring Boot API as an OAuth2 resource server\\n\\nA REST API should not redirect anyone. It accepts `Authorization: Bearer <access token>`, checks the JWT signature against Casdoor\'s JWKS, and checks the claims. Add `spring-boot-starter-web`, `spring-boot-starter-security` and `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server`:\\n\\n```yaml title=\\"application.yml\\"\\nserver:\\n  port: 9000\\n\\nspring:\\n  security:\\n    oauth2:\\n      resourceserver:\\n        jwt:\\n          issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n          jwk-set-uri: https://door.example.com/.well-known/jwks\\n          audiences: <client-id>\\n```\\n\\nWith only this file, Spring Boot builds a `JwtDecoder` that checks the signature, the expiry, `iss` and `aud`. Setting `jwk-set-uri` next to `issuer-uri` means the keys are fetched directly instead of through discovery, and the issuer is still validated. The audience is the client ID of the Casdoor application that issued the token: if a single-page app or the gateway from section 3 obtains the token, use that application\'s client ID.\\n\\nThere is one Casdoor-specific check worth adding. Casdoor signs ID tokens and access tokens with the same key and gives both the client ID as audience, so the default validators would also accept an ID token sent as a bearer token. Every Casdoor token has a `tokenType` claim (`access-token`, `id-token` or `refresh-token`), which makes the check simple. Defining a `JwtDecoder` bean replaces Boot\'s, so the bean re-adds the issuer and audience checks:\\n\\n```java title=\\"ApiSecurityConfig.java\\"\\n@Configuration\\n@EnableWebSecurity\\npublic class ApiSecurityConfig {\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    JwtDecoder jwtDecoder(\\n            @Value(\\"${spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.issuer-uri}\\") String issuer,\\n            @Value(\\"${spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.jwk-set-uri}\\") String jwkSetUri,\\n            @Value(\\"${spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.audiences}\\") String clientId) {\\n        NimbusJwtDecoder decoder = NimbusJwtDecoder.withJwkSetUri(jwkSetUri).build();\\n        decoder.setJwtValidator(new DelegatingOAuth2TokenValidator<>(\\n                JwtValidators.createDefaultWithIssuer(issuer),\\n                new JwtClaimValidator<List<String>>(JwtClaimNames.AUD,\\n                        aud -> aud != null && aud.contains(clientId)),\\n                new JwtClaimValidator<String>(\\"tokenType\\", \\"access-token\\"::equals)));\\n        return decoder;\\n    }\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    SecurityFilterChain apiFilterChain(HttpSecurity http, JwtDecoder jwtDecoder) throws Exception {\\n        http\\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/actuator/health\\").permitAll()\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/api/admin/**\\").hasRole(\\"admin\\")\\n                .anyRequest().authenticated())\\n            .oauth2ResourceServer(server -> server\\n                .jwt(jwt -> jwt\\n                    .decoder(jwtDecoder)\\n                    .jwtAuthenticationConverter(casdoorJwtConverter())))\\n            .sessionManagement(session -> session\\n                .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))\\n            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable());\\n        return http.build();\\n    }\\n\\n    private JwtAuthenticationConverter casdoorJwtConverter() {\\n        JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter scopes = new JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter();\\n        JwtAuthenticationConverter converter = new JwtAuthenticationConverter();\\n        converter.setPrincipalClaimName(\\"name\\");\\n        converter.setJwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter(jwt -> {\\n            Collection<GrantedAuthority> authorities = new ArrayList<>(scopes.convert(jwt));\\n            if (jwt.getClaims().get(\\"roles\\") instanceof Collection<?> roles) {\\n                for (Object role : roles) {\\n                    Object name = role instanceof Map<?, ?> map ? map.get(\\"name\\") : role;\\n                    if (name instanceof String roleName && !roleName.isEmpty()) {\\n                        authorities.add(new SimpleGrantedAuthority(\\"ROLE_\\" + roleName));\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n            return authorities;\\n        });\\n        return converter;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe converter keeps the `SCOPE_` authorities, adds `ROLE_` authorities from the `roles` claim whether it holds names or objects, and uses the `name` claim (the Casdoor user name) as the principal. If the application uses the `JWT-Standard` token format, use `preferred_username` as the principal claim instead. Disabling CSRF and sessions is right here because the API never sees a browser cookie; do not copy those two lines into the web app.\\n\\nTo try it without a browser, get a machine token with the client credentials grant and call the API:\\n\\n```shell\\nTOKEN=$(curl -s -u \'<client-id>:<client-secret>\' \\\\\\n  -d grant_type=client_credentials \\\\\\n  https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/access_token | jq -r .access_token)\\n\\ncurl -H \\"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\\" http://localhost:9000/api/hello\\n```\\n\\nA client credentials token represents the application, not a user, so it has no roles. For more on which claims to check and why, see [JWT validation](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/jwt-validation) and [OAuth scopes](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/oauth-scopes).\\n\\n## 3. Spring Cloud Gateway with TokenRelay\\n\\nWith microservices, a common layout is to let the gateway handle login and keep the services as resource servers. The browser only ever talks to the gateway and holds a session cookie; the gateway stores the user\'s access token and the `TokenRelay` filter adds it as `Authorization: Bearer ...` to each proxied request. The services from section 2 validate it as usual.\\n\\nAdd `spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux` and `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client`, with the Spring Cloud 2025.0 BOM:\\n\\n```yaml title=\\"application.yml\\"\\nserver:\\n  port: 8080\\n\\nspring:\\n  application:\\n    name: gateway\\n  security:\\n    oauth2:\\n      client:\\n        registration:\\n          casdoor:\\n            client-id: <client-id>\\n            client-secret: <client-secret>\\n            authorization-grant-type: authorization_code\\n            scope: openid,profile,email\\n        provider:\\n          casdoor:\\n            issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n            user-name-attribute: preferred_username\\n  cloud:\\n    gateway:\\n      server:\\n        webflux:\\n          routes:\\n            - id: orders\\n              uri: http://localhost:9000\\n              predicates:\\n                - Path=/api/orders/**\\n              filters:\\n                - TokenRelay=\\n            - id: users\\n              uri: http://localhost:9001\\n              predicates:\\n                - Path=/api/users/**\\n              filters:\\n                - TokenRelay=\\n```\\n\\n`TokenRelay=` with no argument relays the token of the signed-in user. The `TokenRelay` filter factory is only created when `spring.security.oauth2.client.*` is configured, and the default authorized-client store is in memory, so a gateway with several replicas needs a shared store or sticky sessions. The services behind it set `audiences` to the gateway\'s client ID.\\n\\nSpring Cloud Gateway runs on WebFlux, so its security configuration is a `SecurityWebFilterChain` built from `ServerHttpSecurity`, not the servlet `SecurityFilterChain`:\\n\\n```java title=\\"GatewaySecurityConfig.java\\"\\n@Configuration\\n@EnableWebFluxSecurity\\npublic class GatewaySecurityConfig {\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    SecurityWebFilterChain gatewayFilterChain(ServerHttpSecurity http,\\n            ReactiveClientRegistrationRepository clientRegistrations) {\\n        OidcClientInitiatedServerLogoutSuccessHandler oidcLogout =\\n                new OidcClientInitiatedServerLogoutSuccessHandler(clientRegistrations);\\n        oidcLogout.setPostLogoutRedirectUri(\\"{baseUrl}/\\");\\n\\n        http\\n            .authorizeExchange(exchanges -> exchanges\\n                .pathMatchers(\\"/\\", \\"/actuator/health\\").permitAll()\\n                .anyExchange().authenticated())\\n            .oauth2Login(Customizer.withDefaults())\\n            .logout(logout -> logout.logoutSuccessHandler(oidcLogout));\\n        return http.build();\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nKeep authorization decisions in the services, where the roles are in the token; the gateway only makes sure there is a signed-in user. CSRF protection stays on in the gateway because it is cookie-based.\\n\\nThe existing [Spring Cloud Gateway](/docs/integration/java/spring-cloud-gateway) and [Spring Cloud](/docs/integration/java/spring-cloud) pages show a different design, where a custom `GlobalFilter` checks a Casdoor user stored in the gateway session.\\n\\n## Alternative: the Casdoor Spring Boot starter\\n\\nIf you would rather not write the YAML, [casdoor-spring-boot-starter](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-spring-boot-starter) 2.x (Spring Boot 3 and 4, Java 17+) takes `casdoor.endpoint`, `casdoor.client-id`, `casdoor.client-secret`, `casdoor.organization-name` and `casdoor.application-name`, and sets the `spring.security.oauth2.*` properties above for whichever Security starter is on the classpath. It registers the client as `casdoor`, maps Casdoor roles to `ROLE_` authorities for both OAuth2 login and the servlet resource server, and exposes the [Casdoor Java SDK](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-java-sdk) services as beans. Its properties have the lowest precedence, so you can still override any of them. See [Spring Boot](/docs/integration/java/spring-boot) for the setup.\\n\\n## Common errors\\n\\n- **\\"The Issuer ... provided in the configuration metadata did not match the requested issuer\\"**: the `issuer-uri` differs from what Casdoor reports. Remove any trailing slash, and set `origin` in Casdoor if the hostnames differ.\\n- **`redirect_uri` rejected by Casdoor**: the callback is not in the application\'s Redirect URLs. Check the scheme and port, especially behind a reverse proxy.\\n- **\\"Missing attribute \'preferred_username\'\\"**: the `profile` scope was not requested, or the application\'s token fields leave out `Name`.\\n- **401 with an `invalid_token` error on the API**: the token came from another application (wrong `aud`), it is expired, or it is an ID token.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- Casdoor discovery document and endpoints: [`object/wellknown_oidc_discovery.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/wellknown_oidc_discovery.go)\\n- Casdoor token claims: [`object/token_jwt.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/token_jwt.go)\\n- [Spring Boot 3.5 reference: Spring Security, OAuth2 client and resource server](https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/3.5/reference/web/spring-security.html)\\n- [Spring Security: OIDC client-initiated logout](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/oauth2/login/logout.html)\\n- [Spring Security: OAuth 2.0 resource server JWT](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/oauth2/resource-server/jwt.html)\\n- [Spring Cloud Gateway: TokenRelay GatewayFilter factory](https://docs.spring.io/spring-cloud-gateway/reference/spring-cloud-gateway-server-webflux/gatewayfilter-factories/tokenrelay-factory.html)\\n- [Spring Cloud Gateway: starter `spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux`](https://docs.spring.io/spring-cloud-gateway/reference/spring-cloud-gateway-server-webflux/starter.html)\\n- [Spring Cloud 2025.0 release notes](https://github.com/spring-cloud/spring-cloud-release/wiki/Spring-Cloud-2025.0-Release-Notes)\\n- [casdoor-spring-boot-starter](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-spring-boot-starter)"},{"id":"casdoor-v4","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-v4","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-30-casdoor-v4.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-30-casdoor-v4.mdx","title":"Casdoor v4: Eine neue Konsole, ein Marktplatz und OAuth f\xfcr MCP-Server","description":"Casdoor v4.0 erschien am 1. September. Zwei Monate und 25 Releases sp\xe4ter sind wir bei v4.25, daher ist dies ein guter Zeitpunkt, um zur\xfcckzublicken, was sich ge\xe4ndert hat. Kurz gesagt: Die Admin-Konsole wurde neu aufgebaut, das Self-Hosting ist deutlich weniger fummelig geworden, es gibt nun einen Marketplace f\xfcr Integrationen und Anmelde-Themes, und Casdoor kann als Autorisierungsserver f\xfcr MCP-Server und KI-Agenten fungieren.","date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/release"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"MCP","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/mcp"},{"inline":true,"label":"Self-hosting","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/self-hosting"}],"readingTime":4.87,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-v4","title":"Casdoor v4: Eine neue Konsole, ein Marktplatz und OAuth f\xfcr MCP-Server","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Release","IAM","SSO","OAuth","MCP","Self-hosting"],"date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Spring Boot SSO with OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server and Spring Cloud Gateway with Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc"},"nextItem":{"title":"Casdoor auf Kubernetes betreiben: Offizielles Helm Chart und Configuration as Code","permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes"}},"content":"Casdoor v4.0 erschien am 1. September. Zwei Monate und 25 Releases sp\xe4ter sind wir bei v4.25, daher ist dies ein guter Zeitpunkt, um zur\xfcckzublicken, was sich ge\xe4ndert hat. Kurz gesagt: Die Admin-Konsole wurde neu aufgebaut, das Self-Hosting ist deutlich weniger fummelig geworden, es gibt nun einen Marketplace f\xfcr Integrationen und Anmelde-Themes, und Casdoor kann als Autorisierungsserver f\xfcr MCP-Server und KI-Agenten fungieren.\\n\\nFalls Sie es noch nicht verwendet haben: Casdoor ist eine Open-Source-Plattform f\xfcr Identit\xe4ts- und Zugriffsverwaltung, geschrieben in Go. Es ist ein vollst\xe4ndiger Identity Provider, keine Bibliothek oder ein Login-Proxy. Benutzer existieren in Casdoor, Casdoor stellt die Tokens aus, und Ihre Apps kommunizieren \xfcber OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect, SAML 2.0, CAS, LDAP oder SCIM damit. Die Autorisierung wird von [Apache Casbin](https://casbin.apache.org) gehandhabt, sodass ACL-, RBAC- und ABAC-Modelle alle funktionieren. Es steht unter der Apache-2.0-Lizenz und ist im CNCF Landscape gelistet.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Eine neu aufgebaute Konsole und Anmeldeseiten {#a-rebuilt-console-and-sign-in-pages}\\n\\nv4.0 ersetzte die Ant Design Admin-Konsole durch eine neue, die auf shadcn/ui, Tailwind und Vite (jetzt mit React 19) basiert. Sie verf\xfcgt \xfcber eine Befehlspalette, Bulk-Zeilenauswahl, Inline-Formularvalidierung, zweispaltige Formulare und Tabellen, in denen Spalten ausgeblendet werden k\xf6nnen. Der Sprachwechsel l\xe4dt die Seite nicht mehr neu.\\n\\nDie Seiten f\xfcr Anmeldung, Registrierung und Passwort-Reset wurden ebenfalls neu gestaltet. Der Dark Mode funktioniert ordnungsgem\xe4\xdf, und die Seiten sind auf dem Smartphone nutzbar.\\n\\nWenn Sie von v3 upgraden, lesen Sie zuerst den Upgrade-Leitfaden, der in der README verlinkt ist. Das Wichtigste, was Sie pr\xfcfen m\xfcssen, ist der Pfad des Frontend-Builds, wenn Sie statische Dateien \xfcber ein CDN oder einen Reverse Proxy bereitstellen.\\n\\n## Einfacheres Self-Hosting {#easier-to-self-host}\\n\\nViele der Arbeiten an v4 entstanden aus Issues und Forum-Threads von Personen, die Casdoor selbst betreiben:\\n\\n- **Kein Standard-Passwort.** Beim ersten Besuch fragt Casdoor nach dem `admin`-Passwort, oder Sie k\xf6nnen es mit `initAdminPassword` festlegen.\\n- **Bereitstellung unter Sub-Pfad.** Setzen Sie `basePath`, um Casdoor unter `https://example.com/auth/` statt auf einem eigenen Host bereitzustellen.\\n- **Forward Auth.** Casdoor hat einen Forward-Auth-Endpoint f\xfcr Traefik, Caddy und Nginx, sodass Sie Apps ohne eigenes Login dahinter schalten k\xf6nnen, mit \xf6ffentlichen Pfaden und regelspezifischen Einstellungen pro Site.\\n- **Docker-Tags.** Images werden jetzt auch mit `X`, `X.Y` und `X.Y.Z` getaggt, sodass Sie einer Major- oder Minor-Version folgen k\xf6nnen.\\n- **Helm.** Das Chart wurde in das Hauptrepository verschoben, wird bei jedem Release auf GHCR und [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) ver\xf6ffentlicht und kann Organisationen, Anwendungen und Benutzer als Konfiguration enthalten. Siehe [Casdoor auf Kubernetes ausf\xfchren](/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes).\\n- **Windows-Dienst.** Die Binary kann als Windows-Dienst ausgef\xfchrt werden.\\n- **Upgrades.** Die Systeminfo-Seite zeigt die neueste Version an und kann ein offizielles Binary mit einem Klick aktualisieren.\\n- **Betrieb.** Container-Health-Checks, Prometheus-Counter f\xfcr erfolgreiche und fehlgeschlagene Anmeldungen, Audit-Logs, die an Syslog weitergeleitet werden, sowie Redis Cluster-Support.\\n\\n## Marktplatz {#marketplace}\\n\\nDer neue [Marketplace](https://marketplace.casdoor.com) erm\xf6glicht es Administratoren, Integrationen direkt innerhalb von Casdoor zu installieren: Anwendungsvorlagen, Identity Provider und Anmelde-Themes. Bei der Installation wird die Konfiguration automatisch ausgef\xfcllt, und bei der Deinstallation wird der vorherige Zustand wiederhergestellt.\\n\\nEs gibt bisher mehr als 20 Anmelde-Themes. Sie k\xf6nnen ein Theme auf Ihrer eigenen Anmeldeseite vorinstalliert testen. Organisationsadministratoren k\xf6nnen die Anmeldeseiten au\xdferdem mit ihren eigenen Schriftarten, Bildern und HTML anpassen, innerhalb einer Allowlist, die Formulare und Skripte blockiert. Die schreibgesch\xfctzte Demo unter [door.casdoor.com](https://door.casdoor.com) wechselt zwischen den Themes, wenn Sie sie sehen m\xf6chten.\\n\\n## OAuth f\xfcr MCP-Server und KI-Agenten {#oauth-for-mcp-servers-and-ai-agents}\\n\\nDie MCP-Spezifikation verlangt, dass Remote-MCP-Server OAuth verwenden, und MCP-Clients kennen im Voraus normalerweise nicht den Autorisierungsserver, dem sie begegnen werden. Casdoor deckt nun die erforderlichen Komponenten ab:\\n\\n- OAuth 2.1 Authorization Code Flow mit PKCE und eine Consent-Seite, auf der Benutzer nur einige der angeforderten Scopes gew\xe4hren k\xf6nnen\\n- Dynamic Client Registration (RFC 7591) und Client-Management (RFC 7592)\\n- Client ID Metadata Documents: Ein MCP-Client kann eine URL als `client_id` verwenden, ohne sich vorher zu registrieren\\n- `private_key_jwt` Client-Authentifizierung f\xfcr dynamisch registrierte Clients\\n- Resource Indicators (RFC 8707) und Protected Resource Metadata (RFC 9728)\\n- Token Exchange (RFC 8693), einschlie\xdflich ID-JAGs f\xfcr unternehmensverwaltete Autorisierung, sodass der IdP einer Organisation entscheiden kann, welche Agenten auf welche MCP-Server zugreifen d\xfcrfen\\n\\nRichten Sie die gesch\xfctzten Ressourcen-Metadaten Ihres MCP-Servers auf Casdoor, und Anmeldung, Consent, Token-Ausstellung und Validierung werden f\xfcr Sie erledigt. Casdoor betreibt auch seinen eigenen MCP-Server, sodass ein Assistent wie Claude Code oder Cursor Benutzer, Anwendungen und Berechtigungen dar\xfcber verwalten kann.\\n\\n## Weitere Protokollarbeiten {#other-protocol-work}\\n\\n- SAML Single Logout und IdP-initiiertes SSO\\n- OIDC `prompt=none`, bessere Konformit\xe4t f\xfcr Hybrid-Flow, `prompt` und `max_age` sowie den RFC 9207 `iss`-Parameter\\n- Token-Widerruf (RFC 7009), DPoP und der Device Authorization Flow\\n- Magic Link-Anmeldung, Pr\xfcfung der Passwort-Historie und Passkeys, die \xfcber Geschwister-Subdomains geteilt werden\\n- Exklusive Anmeldung mit einer maximalen Anzahl von Sitzungen pro Benutzer\\n\\n## Ausprobieren {#try-it}\\n\\nDer schnellste Weg, sich umzusehen:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -p 8000:8000 casbin/casdoor-all-in-one\\n```\\n\\n\xd6ffnen Sie dann http://localhost:8000. Das Image enth\xe4lt SQLite und Beispieldaten, verwenden Sie es also zum Ausprobieren, nicht f\xfcr Produktion. Der [Installationsleitfaden](/docs/basic/server-installation) behandelt reale Bereitstellungen.\\n\\nEs gibt zwei Online-Demos: [demo.casdoor.com](https://demo.casdoor.com) ist beschreibbar und setzt sich alle f\xfcnf Minuten zur\xfcck, und [door.casdoor.com](https://door.casdoor.com) ist schreibgesch\xfctzt.\\n\\nWenn Sie es lieber nicht selbst betreiben m\xf6chten, bietet Ihnen [Casdoor Cloud](https://www.casdoor.com/pricing) eine dedizierte, verwaltete Instanz zu einem Festpreis ohne Geb\xfchren pro Benutzer. Neue Konten erhalten 10 $ Guthaben zum Testen.\\n\\n## Links\\n\\n- GitHub: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor\\n- Release Notes: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/releases\\n- Docs: https://casdoor.ai/docs/overview\\n- Discord: https://discord.gg/5rPsrAzK7S\\n\\nFehlerberichte und Funktionsw\xfcnsche sind auf GitHub willkommen. Wenn Casdoor f\xfcr Sie n\xfctzlich ist, hilft ein Stern anderen, es zu finden."},{"id":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","title":"Casdoor auf Kubernetes betreiben: Offizielles Helm Chart und Configuration as Code","description":"Casdoor verf\xfcgt \xfcber ein offizielles Helm Chart. Es befindet sich im Hauptrepository unter manifests/casdoor, wird bei jedem Casdoor-Release im GHCR ver\xf6ffentlicht und ist auf Artifact Hub gelistet. Ab Casdoor v4.18.0 kann das Chart auch Ihre Organisationen, Anwendungen, Benutzer und Provider als deklarative Konfiguration enthalten: \xc4ndern Sie diese in der values-Datei, f\xfchren Sie helm upgrade aus, und die laufenden Pods wenden die \xc4nderung ohne Neustart an.","date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Kubernetes","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/kubernetes"},{"inline":true,"label":"Helm","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/helm"},{"inline":true,"label":"GitOps","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/git-ops"},{"inline":true,"label":"Configuration as Code","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/configuration-as-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":5.76,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","title":"Casdoor auf Kubernetes betreiben: Offizielles Helm Chart und Configuration as Code","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Kubernetes","Helm","GitOps","Configuration as Code","IAM","SSO"],"date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor v4: Eine neue Konsole, ein Marktplatz und OAuth f\xfcr MCP-Server","permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-v4"},"nextItem":{"title":"Casdoor tritt der CNCF Cloud Native Landscape bei","permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"}},"content":"Casdoor verf\xfcgt \xfcber ein offizielles Helm Chart. Es befindet sich im Hauptrepository unter [`manifests/casdoor`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor), wird bei jedem Casdoor-Release im GHCR ver\xf6ffentlicht und ist auf [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) gelistet. Ab Casdoor v4.18.0 kann das Chart auch Ihre Organisationen, Anwendungen, Benutzer und Provider als deklarative Konfiguration enthalten: \xc4ndern Sie diese in der values-Datei, f\xfchren Sie `helm upgrade` aus, und die laufenden Pods wenden die \xc4nderung ohne Neustart an.\\n\\nDieser Beitrag f\xfchrt durch eine Kubernetes-Bereitstellung vom ersten `helm install` bis hin zu einem Setup, das Sie in Git speichern k\xf6nnen.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Installation {#install}\\n\\nSie ben\xf6tigen Helm v3.8+ und einen Cluster:\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor\\n```\\n\\nDie Chart-Version entspricht der Casdoor-Version, daher installiert `--version 4.18.0` Casdoor 4.18.0. Ohne Ingress oder Gateway ist der Dienst nur innerhalb des Clusters erreichbar. Um es auszuprobieren, leiten Sie einen Port weiter:\\n\\n```shell\\nkubectl port-forward svc/casdoor 8000:8000\\n```\\n\\n\xd6ffnen Sie dann `http://localhost:8000`. Beim ersten Besuch fragt Casdoor Sie nach dem Passwort f\xfcr `built-in/admin`; melden Sie sich dann damit an. Siehe [Anmeldung in der Admin-Konsole](/docs/basic/server-installation#sign-in).\\n\\n## Eine Produktionsumgebung {#a-production-setup}\\n\\nDie Standardeinstellungen sind f\xfcr einen schnellen Test gedacht: Casdoor speichert seine Daten in SQLite im Container, sodass sie verloren gehen, wenn der Pod ersetzt wird. F\xfcr eine echte Bereitstellung legen Sie die folgenden Einstellungen in einer `values.yaml`-Datei ab und installieren Sie mit `helm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor -f values.yaml`.\\n\\n### Externe Datenbank {#external-database}\\n\\n```yaml\\ndatabase:\\n  driver: postgres # or mysql, cockroachdb\\n  host: postgres.db.svc.cluster.local\\n  user: casdoor\\n  password: change-me\\n  databaseName: casdoor\\n  sslMode: require\\n```\\n\\nUm das Datenbankpasswort nicht in der values-Datei abzulegen, setzen Sie die gesamte `app.conf` in ein Secret und definieren Sie `configFromSecret` mit dessen Namen.\\n\\n### Casdoor verf\xfcgbar machen {#exposing-casdoor}\\n\\nDas Chart unterst\xfctzt sowohl ein klassisches Ingress als auch die Gateway API. Mit einem Ingress-Controller und cert-manager:\\n\\n```yaml\\ningress:\\n  enabled: true\\n  className: nginx\\n  annotations:\\n    cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: letsencrypt-prod\\n  hosts:\\n    - host: door.example.com\\n      paths:\\n        - path: /\\n          pathType: Prefix\\n  tls:\\n    - secretName: casdoor-tls\\n      hosts:\\n        - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nMit der Gateway API (Istio, Envoy Gateway, Cilium, Kong, NGINX Gateway Fabric und andere) h\xe4ngen Sie eine `HTTPRoute` an ein vorhandenes Gateway an:\\n\\n```yaml\\ngatewayApi:\\n  enabled: true\\n  parentRefs:\\n    - name: my-gateway\\n      namespace: gateway-system\\n  hostnames:\\n    - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nDas Chart kann auch das Gateway selbst erstellen und eine HTTP-zu-HTTPS-Umleitung hinzuf\xfcgen, siehe den [Helm-Leitfaden](/docs/basic/try-with-helm#exposing-casdoor).\\n\\n### Mehr als eine Replik {#more-than-one-replica}\\n\\nCasdoor speichert Anmelde-Sitzungen standardm\xe4\xdfig lokal auf der Festplatte. Bevor Sie mehr als eine Replik betreiben, verweisen Sie alle auf denselben Redis, damit ein Benutzer angemeldet bleibt, unabh\xe4ngig davon, welcher Pod die Anfrage bearbeitet. Jede `app.conf`-Einstellung kann als Umgebungsvariable \xfcbergeben werden, sodass der Endpunkt aus einem Secret stammen kann:\\n\\n```yaml\\nreplicaCount: 3\\n\\nenvFromSecret:\\n  - name: redisEndpoint\\n    secretName: casdoor-redis\\n    key: endpoint # host:port, or host:port,db,password\\n```\\n\\nDerselbe Redis wird f\xfcr den Status des OAuth-Ger\xe4teflusses verwendet. Sobald Redis eingerichtet ist, f\xfcgt `autoscaling.enabled: true` einen HorizontalPodAutoscaler hinzu.\\n\\n## Configuration as Code {#configuration-as-code}\\n\\nOrganisationen und Anwendungen in der Web-Oberfl\xe4che zusammenzuklicken ist f\xfcr einen ersten Test in Ordnung, aber die meisten Teams m\xf6chten das Setup ihres Identit\xe4tsanbieters wie den Rest ihrer Infrastruktur \xfcberpr\xfcfen und versionieren. Die `initData`-Werte tun genau das:\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  data:\\n    organizations:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: acme\\n        displayName: Acme\\n        passwordType: bcrypt\\n    applications:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: app-acme-portal\\n        organization: acme\\n        displayName: Acme Portal\\n        redirectUris:\\n          - https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\n    users:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: alice\\n        displayName: Alice\\n        password: change-me\\n        signupApplication: app-acme-portal\\n    roles:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: admins\\n        displayName: Admins\\n        users: [acme/alice]\\n        isEnabled: true\\n```\\n\\nDas Chart speichert die Daten in einem Secret und bindet sie in die Pods ein. Casdoor wendet sie beim Start an und pr\xfcft sie dann alle 30 Sekunden (`initData.watchInterval`). Wenn Sie die Daten \xe4ndern und `helm upgrade` ausf\xfchren, aktualisiert kubelet das eingebundene Secret, und die laufenden Pods wenden es ohne Neustart an.\\n\\nEinige Regeln machen die parallele Nutzung neben der Web-Oberfl\xe4che sicher:\\n\\n- **Nur die von Ihnen geschriebenen Felder werden verwaltet.** Ein bereits vorhandenes Objekt erh\xe4lt die in der Datei festgelegten Felder; seine anderen Felder behalten die Werte bei, die in der Benutzeroberfl\xe4che bearbeitet wurden. Sie k\xf6nnen nur die Redirect URIs einer Anwendung verwalten und den Rest der Admin-Konsole \xfcberlassen.\\n- **Passw\xf6rter sind initiale Passw\xf6rter.** Das `password` eines Benutzers wird nur bei der Erstellung des Benutzers verwendet, sodass Benutzer ihr Passwort danach \xe4ndern k\xf6nnen.\\n- **Nichts wird gel\xf6scht.** Wenn ein Objekt aus der Datei entfernt wird, wird es nicht aus Casdoor entfernt.\\n- **Fehler bringen Casdoor nicht zum Absturz.** Wenn die Anwendung eines Objekts fehlschl\xe4gt, wird der Fehler protokolliert und Casdoor bleibt im Dienst; die Daten werden bei jeder \xdcberpr\xfcfung erneut versucht, bis sie angewendet werden.\\n\\nDas Dateiformat ist dasjenige, das Casdoor seit jeher f\xfcr [Dateninitialisierung](/docs/deployment/data-initialization) verwendet: Organisationen, Anwendungen, Benutzer, Anbieter, Zertifikate, Rollen, Berechtigungen, Gruppen, LDAP-Server, Webhooks, Pl\xe4ne, Preise und mehr, mit denselben Feldnamen wie die REST-API. Ein schneller Einstiegsweg besteht darin, eine bestehende Instanz mit `casdoor -export` zu exportieren und die Objekte beizubehalten, die verwaltet werden sollen.\\n\\nUm die Daten aus der values-Datei herauszuhalten, z. B. wenn sie von Sealed Secrets oder External Secrets verwaltet wird, erstellen Sie das Secret selbst:\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  existingSecret: casdoor-init-data\\n  existingSecretKey: init_data.yaml\\n```\\n\\nDieselbe Funktion funktioniert auch ohne Kubernetes. Mounten Sie eine JSON- oder YAML-Datei und setzen Sie:\\n\\n```ini\\ninitDataFile = /init-data/init_data.yaml\\ninitDataMerge = true\\ninitDataWatchInterval = 30\\n```\\n\\n## Oder verwenden Sie Terraform {#or-use-terraform}\\n\\nWenn Sie `terraform plan`, Importe vorhandener Objekte und Drift-Erkennung bevorzugen, verwaltet der offizielle [Terraform-Anbieter](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/casdoor/casdoor) dieselben Objekte \xfcber die Casdoor-API:\\n\\n```terraform\\nresource \\"casdoor_application\\" \\"portal\\" {\\n  name          = \\"app-acme-portal\\"\\n  organization  = \\"acme\\"\\n  redirect_uris = [\\"https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\"]\\n}\\n```\\n\\nVerwenden Sie Helm values, wenn die Konfiguration zusammen mit dem Deployment ausgeliefert werden soll, und Terraform, wenn Casdoor eines von vielen Systemen ist, die Sie bereits damit verwalten. Siehe den [Terraform-Leitfaden](/docs/deployment/terraform).\\n\\n## Migration vom alten Chart {#coming-from-the-old-chart}\\n\\nBis v4.15.0 wurde das Chart als `oci://registry-1.docker.io/casbin/casdoor-helm-charts` ver\xf6ffentlicht, und das separate Repository `casdoor/casdoor-helm` ist nun archiviert. Das Docker-Hub-Chart erh\xe4lt weiterhin jede Ver\xf6ffentlichung, aber neue Installationen sollten das GHCR-Chart verwenden. Die Migration einer bestehenden Release ist ein regul\xe4res Upgrade, und die Ressourcennamen bleiben gleich:\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm upgrade casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor --version <version>\\n```\\n\\n## Links\\n\\n- Helm chart: [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [source](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor)\\n- Guides: [Try with Helm](/docs/basic/try-with-helm) \xb7 [Data initialization and configuration as code](/docs/deployment/data-initialization#configuration-as-code) \xb7 [Terraform](/docs/deployment/terraform)\\n- Sie m\xf6chten es nicht selbst betreiben? [Casdoor Cloud](https://www.casdoor.com/pricing?utm_source=casdoor.ai&utm_medium=blog&utm_content=kubernetes) bietet Ihnen eine dedizierte Casdoor-Instanz, die wir hosten und f\xfcr Sie aktuell halten."},{"id":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","title":"Casdoor tritt der CNCF Cloud Native Landscape bei","description":"Am 22. Februar 2026 ist Casdoor der CNCF Cloud Native Landscape beigetreten, in der Kategorie Security & Compliance unter Provisioning. Die Landscape vereint weit verbreitete cloud-native Projekte und Produkte; die Aufnahme hilft Nutzern und Unternehmen, L\xf6sungen f\xfcr den Aufbau cloud-nativer Systeme zu entdecken und zu bewerten.","date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"CNCF","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/cncf"},{"inline":true,"label":"Cloud Native","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/cloud-native"},{"inline":true,"label":"Landscape","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/landscape"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Security","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/security"}],"readingTime":2.02,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","title":"Casdoor tritt der CNCF Cloud Native Landscape bei","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","CNCF","Cloud Native","Landscape","IAM","SSO","Security"],"date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor auf Kubernetes betreiben: Offizielles Helm Chart und Configuration as Code","permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes"},"nextItem":{"title":"Verbesserung der App-Sicherheit und Benutzererfahrung mit Casdoor Face Authentifizierung SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"Am 22. Februar 2026 ist **Casdoor** der [CNCF Cloud Native Landscape](https://landscape.cncf.io/) beigetreten, in der Kategorie **Security & Compliance** unter Provisioning. Die Landscape vereint weit verbreitete cloud-native Projekte und Produkte; die Aufnahme hilft Nutzern und Unternehmen, L\xf6sungen f\xfcr den Aufbau cloud-nativer Systeme zu entdecken und zu bewerten.\\n\\n[https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<img src=\\"/img/blog/cncf-landscape-casdoor.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor on CNCF Landscape - Security and Compliance section\\" style={{maxWidth: \'100%\', height: \'auto\', border: \'1px solid #eee\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n\\n_Casdoor on the CNCF Cloud Native Landscape (Security & Compliance)_\\n\\n## \xdcber Casdoor {#about-casdoor}\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/casdoor-logo_1185x256.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nCasdoor ist eine Open-Source-, UI-first Identity and Access Management (IAM) / Single Sign-On (SSO)-Plattform mit einer Web-Benutzeroberfl\xe4che. Sie unterst\xfctzt OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML und CAS sowie viele Social Logins (Google, GitHub, WeChat usw.) und hilft Ihnen, Benutzermanagement und SSO in wenigen Schritten aufzubauen. Casdoor wird von [Casbin](https://casbin.org) in Golang und React entwickelt. Es nutzt eine Architektur mit Trennung von Frontend und Backend, unterst\xfctzt hohe Nebenl\xe4ufigkeit und wird aktiv von der Community gepflegt. Das Projekt hat \xfcber 13k GitHub-Sterne erhalten.\\n\\n## \xdcber CNCF und die Cloud Native Landscape {#about-cncf-and-the-cloud-native-landscape}\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1.5rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://www.cncf.io/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/cncf-main-site-logo.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF - Cloud Native Computing Foundation\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n<img src=\\"https://landscape.cncf.io/images/logo_header.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF Cloud Native Landscape\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nDie **Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF)** ist ein Linux-Foundation-Projekt, das cloud-native Technologien f\xf6rdert. Ihre Vision ist es, cloud native allgegenw\xe4rtig zu machen. Die CNCF pflegt die **Cloud Native Landscape** ([https://landscape.cncf.io](https://landscape.cncf.io)), die Projekte und Produkte im cloud-native \xd6kosystem klassifiziert und verfolgt und als Referenzkarte f\xfcr Entwickler und Unternehmen dient.\\n\\n## Ausblick {#looking-ahead}\\n\\nDer Beitritt zur CNCF Landscape spiegelt Fortschritte in der Reife und Community von Casdoor wider. Wir werden Casdoor und seine Integration in cloud-native Workflows weiterhin verbessern und freuen uns \xfcber weitere Nutzer und Mitwirkende, die der Community beitreten.\\n\\nVerwandte Links:\\n\\n- [Casdoor](https://casdoor.org) \xb7 [GitHub](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [Dokumentation](https://casdoor.org/docs) \xb7 [CNCF Landscape \u2013 Casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?selected=casdoor)"},{"id":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","title":"Verbesserung der App-Sicherheit und Benutzererfahrung mit Casdoor Face Authentifizierung SSO","description":"In einer Zeit, in der digitales Identit\xe4tsmanagement von entscheidender Bedeutung ist, revolutionieren Single Sign-On (SSO) L\xf6sungen wie Casdoor die Interaktion zwischen Nutzern und verschiedenen Anwendungen. Das SSO von Casdoor vereinfacht den Login-Prozess, indem es Benutzern erm\xf6glicht, sich nur einmal zu authentifizieren, um auf mehrere Dienste zuzugreifen. Dies erh\xf6ht nicht nur die Benutzerfreundlichkeit, sondern erh\xf6ht auch das Engagement und die Zufriedenheit.","date":"2023-12-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Single-Sign-On","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/single-sign-on"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face Recogniztion","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/face-recogniztion"},{"inline":true,"label":"FaceID","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/face-id"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/face"},{"inline":true,"label":"Machine Learning","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/machine-learning"}],"readingTime":1.76,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"James Lawson","title":"Product Manager","url":"https://casdoor.org","imageURL":"https://cdn.casbin.com/casdoor/static/admin.png","key":"James Lawson","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","title":"Verbesserung der App-Sicherheit und Benutzererfahrung mit Casdoor Face Authentifizierung SSO","authors":["James Lawson"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Single-Sign-On","Face Recogniztion","FaceID","Face","Machine Learning"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor tritt der CNCF Cloud Native Landscape bei","permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"},"nextItem":{"title":"Willkommen","permalink":"/de/blog/welcome"}},"content":"In einer Zeit, in der digitales Identit\xe4tsmanagement von entscheidender Bedeutung ist, revolutionieren Single Sign-On (SSO) L\xf6sungen wie Casdoor die Interaktion zwischen Nutzern und verschiedenen Anwendungen. Das SSO von Casdoor vereinfacht den Login-Prozess, indem es Benutzern erm\xf6glicht, sich nur einmal zu authentifizieren, um auf mehrere Dienste zuzugreifen. Dies erh\xf6ht nicht nur die Benutzerfreundlichkeit, sondern erh\xf6ht auch das Engagement und die Zufriedenheit.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nF\xfcr Unternehmen optimiert das SSO die Verwaltung von Benutzeranmeldungen, erh\xf6ht die Sicherheit und vereinfacht neue Benutzer an Bord. Durch die Zentralisierung der Benutzerverwaltung kann das Risiko mit schwachen oder recycelten Passw\xf6rtern gedrosselt werden.\\n\\nAngesichts wachsender Online-Bedrohungen ist jedoch die Integration robuster Sicherheitsma\xdfnahmen in SSO-Systemen wie Casdoor unerl\xe4sslich. Hier kommt die Gesichtauthentifizierungstechnologie ins Spiel, indem sie eine zus\xe4tzliche Schutzschicht bei der Aufrechterhaltung benutzerfreundlicher Interaktionen hinzuf\xfcgt.\\n\\nFace Authentifizierung verwendet einzigartige Gesichtsmerkmale, um Benutzeridentit\xe4ten zu best\xe4tigen. Es vergleicht Live-Bilder mit gespeicherten Gesichtsvorlagen und bietet eine sichere und unkomplizierte M\xf6glichkeit, Benutzer zu authentifizieren. Diese Methode zeichnet sich durch hohe Genauigkeit auch unter unterschiedlichen Bedingungen aus und wird in modernen Endger\xe4ten weit verbreitet.\\n\\nDie Integration der Gesichtserkennung in Casdoor SSO-System verspricht eine sichere, effiziente und nahtlose Benutzererfahrung. Um eine optimale Leistung zu gew\xe4hrleisten, ist es von entscheidender Bedeutung, sich auf folgende Bereiche zu konzentrieren:\\n\\n1. _Anti-Spoofing Ma\xdfnahmen_: Die Umsetzung der Lebenserkennung, wie das Aufsp\xfcren von Augenbewegungen oder zuf\xe4lligen Gesten, ist unerl\xe4sslich, um den Fehlversuchen entgegenzuwirken und die Systemintegrit\xe4t zu wahren.\\n\\n2. _Regelm\xe4\xdfige Updates zur Verbesserung der Genauigkeit_: Die Aktualisierung der Gesicht-Authentifizierungstechnologie mit den neuesten KI und biometrischen Fortschritten gew\xe4hrleistet Genauigkeit und verbessert das Nutzererlebnis. Es ist wichtig, verschiedene Datens\xe4tze f\xfcr Algorithmustraining zu verwenden, um Vorurteile zu vermeiden.\\n\\n3. _Ausgewogenheit von Sicherheit und Bequemlichkeit_: Die besten Systeme bieten eine perfekte Balance zwischen strengen Sicherheitsma\xdfnahmen und Benutzerfreundlichkeit. Dieser Ansatz ermutigt die Benutzer zu adoptieren, indem er einen einfachen Authentifizierungsprozess sicherstellt, ohne die Sicherheit zu beeintr\xe4chtigen.\\n\\nZusammenfassend l\xe4sst sich sagen, dass Unternehmen durch die Integration der Gesichtserkennung in das SSO von Casdoor ein sicheres, effizientes und benutzerfreundliches Anmeldeerlebnis anbieten k\xf6nnen. Dies st\xe4rkt nicht nur die Sicherheit, sondern passt sich auch den Erwartungen der modernen Nutzer in der digitalen Welt an."},{"id":"welcome","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/welcome","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","title":"Willkommen","description":"Willkommen auf der offiziellen Casdoor Webseite!\u{1F44B}","date":"2021-08-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Authentication","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/authentication"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth2.0","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/o-auth-2-0"},{"inline":true,"label":"Casbin","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casbin"}],"readingTime":1.52,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"ErikQQY","title":"Casbin Member","url":"https://github.com/ErikQQY","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52615090?s=120&v=4","key":"ErikQQY","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"welcome","title":"Willkommen","authors":["ErikQQY"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Authentication","OAuth2.0","Casbin"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Verbesserung der App-Sicherheit und Benutzererfahrung mit Casdoor Face Authentifizierung SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"Willkommen auf der offiziellen Casdoor Webseite!\u{1F44B}\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Was ist Casdoor? {#what-is-casdoor}\\n\\nCasdoor ist eine UI-first [Identity Access Management (IAM)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Identity_management) / [Single-Sign-On (SSO)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_sign-on)-Plattform, die auf OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML und CAS basiert.\\n\\nCasdoor wird von [Casbin](https://casbin.org) mit Golang und React entwickelt.\\n\\nZiel von Casdoor ist es, eine leistungsstarke Authentifizierungsplattform f\xfcr die Benutzerverwaltung bereitzustellen.\\n\\n## Warum Casdoor? {#why-use-casdoor}\\n\\n* \u26A1 Leistungsstarke Funktion und einfache Konfiguration. With support for  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_google.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_github.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_facebook.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_twitter.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_linkedin.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_weibo.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wechat.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_qq.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_dingtalk.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>   <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitee.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wecom.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img><img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitlab.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> sign in and traditional username/password sign in, Casdoor helps you implement your users management system just in a few steps.\\n\\n* \u{1F4A5} Front-End-Back-End-Trennarchitektur. Casdoor bietet eine man\xf6vrierf\xe4hige Web-Benutzeroberfl\xe4che und unterst\xfctzt eine hohe Parallelit\xe4t.\\n\\n* \u{1F942} Aktive Open Source Community. Casdoor ist Open Source und wird aktiv gepflegt.\\n\\n* \u{1F310} Mehrsprachige Unterst\xfctzung. Casdoor unterst\xfctzt mehrere Sprachen, darunter Deutsch, Chinesisch, Englisch, Franz\xf6sisch und viele mehr.\\n\\n\\n## Wie benutze ich Casdoor? {#how-to-use-casdoor}\\n\\nSie k\xf6nnen dem detaillierten Dokument folgen, um mehr \xfcber die [Casdoor API](https://door.casdoor.com/swagger/) zu erfahren und [Ihr eigenes Casdoor einzurichten](/docs/basic/server-installation). Oder nutzen Sie die [Casdoor SDKs](/docs/how-to-connect/sdk), um Ihre eigene Anwendung zu implementieren."}]}}')}}]);